her precipitation thermal stability particularly alto sinu subregion where vegetation heterogeneity favor greater availability host plant contrast more homogeneou environment such those coastal subregion limit speci richnes reduc variety plant form conclud distribution cassidinae link availability specific food resourc these phytophagou insect which exhibit high host specificity coleoptera habitat heterogeneity multivariate ordination neotropical region seasonality composicion taxonomica ecologia de las especi de cassidinae en cordoba acta biol colomb 31 36-48 mayo agosto 2026 37,1.0.0,db5347daef20282d
0120-548X,123457_Versión_Final.pdf,123457_Versión_Final_0,en,Editor: Angelica Plata Rueda,"The study focused on assessing the stability of 55 F5:8 bread wheat recombinant inbred lines (RILs), along with five locally grown high-yielding check cultivars across multiple locations in Khyber Pakhtunkhwa, Pakistan. Using an alpha lattice design over two years during 2014/15 and 2015/16 researchers analyzed the genotype × environment (GE) interaction and its impact on grain yield. Results from the additive main effect and multiplicative interaction (AMMI) analysis highlighted significant GE interactions influencing grain yield variability. Genotypes, environments, and their interaction contributed 9.0 %, 35.2 %, and 28.9 %, to total explainable phenotypic variation, respectively. Certain genotypes, such as G17, G45, and G40 showed stable average yield across environments, while G58, G53 and G1 excelled in specific environments. GGE biplot analysis identified stable genotypes (e.g., G51, G36, and G53) and grouped environments into mega-environments based on yield performance. The AMMI and GGE biplot analyses explained similar proportions of the genotype × environment interaction (56.1 % and 55.5 %, respectively). Because the two methods have complementary objectives, AMMI was used to investigate GE interaction patterns, whereas GGE biplot facilitated genotype evaluation, mega-environment identification, and visualization of genotype performance, the two methods should be regarded as complementary rather than competing tools for evaluating genotype performance across environments","Genotype by environment interaction, Multi-environment trial (MET), Triticum aestivum L",marcadores,editor angelica plata rueda,study focus assess stability 55 f5 bread wheat recombinant inbr line rils along five locally grown high-yield check cultivar acros multiple location khyber pakhtunkhwa pakistan using alpha lattice design over two year during 2014 15 2015 16 researcher analyz genotype environment ge interaction its impact grain yield result additive main effect multiplicative interaction ammi analysi highlight significant ge interaction influenc grain yield variability genotyp environment their interaction contribut 35 28 total explainable phenotypic variation respectively certain genotyp such g17 g45 g40 show stable average yield acros environment while g58 g53 g1 excell specific environment gge biplot analysi identifi stable genotyp g51 g36 g53 group environment into mega-environment based yield performance ammi gge biplot analys explain similar proportion genotype environment interaction 56 55 respectively because two method have complementary objectiv ammi used investigate ge interaction pattern wherea gge biplot facilitat genotype evaluation mega-environment identification visual genotype performance two method should regard complementary rather than compet tool evaluat genotype performance acros environment,genotype environment interaction multi-environment trial met triticum aestivum,editor angelica plata rueda study focus assess stability 55 f5 bread wheat recombinant inbr line rils along five locally grown high-yield check cultivar acros multiple location khyber pakhtunkhwa pakistan using alpha lattice design over two year during 2014 15 2015 16 researcher analyz genotype environment ge interaction its impact grain yield result additive main effect multiplicative interaction ammi analysi highlight significant ge interaction influenc grain yield variability genotyp environment their interaction contribut 35 28 total explainable phenotypic variation respectively certain genotyp such g17 g45 g40 show stable average yield acros environment while g58 g53 g1 excell specific environment gge biplot analysi identifi stable genotyp g51 g36 g53 group environment into mega-environment based yield performance ammi gge biplot analys explain similar proportion genotype environment interaction 56 55 respectively because two method have complementary objectiv ammi used investigate ge interaction pattern wherea gge biplot facilitat genotype evaluation mega-environment identification visual genotype performance two method should regard complementary rather than compet tool evaluat genotype performance acros environment genotype environment interaction multi-environment trial met triticum aestivum,1.0.0,45942b838d7bcf99
0120-548X,124113_Versión_Final_2026.pdf,124113_Versión_Final_2026_0,en,", Marcus Vinícius Dias-Souza1*","Mimosa pudica and Alpinia officinarum are largely used in different countries to treat infectious diseases as well as digestive disorders and inflammatory conditions. Despite their long history of traditional use, the pharmacological properties of both plants are poorly described, including their antimicrobial potential. This study investigated the effectiveness of the ethanolic extracts of M. pudica and A. officinarum against bacterial clinical isolates. The minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC) and minimal bactericidal concentration (MBC) tests and assessed their anti-inflammatory potential and cytotoxicity in vitro. Qualitative methods were used, and ultra-highperformance liquid chromatography coupled to high-resolution tandem mass spectrometry (UHPLC-HRMS/MS) to investigate the phytochemical properties of the extracts. M. pudica and A. officinarum were active against the clinical isolates of Pseudomonas aeruginosa at 8 µg/mL, and both displayed anti-inflammatory properties. UHPLC-HRMS/MS analysis indicated a total of 24 phytocompounds in M. pudica extract and seven in A. officinarum. Only M. pudica presented an acceptable cytotoxicity index for further studies on the antimicrobial potential of chromatographic fractions and isolated phytomolecules. In addition, the data supports research on the safety of A. officinarum in the context of traditional medicine","Anti-infective agents, botanical extracts, cell death, secondary metabolites",marcadores,marcu viniciu dias-souza1,mimosa pudica alpinia officinarum largely used different countri treat infectiou diseas well digestive disorder inflammatory condition despite their long history traditional use pharmacological properti both plant poorly describ includ their antimicrobial potential study investigat effectivenes ethanolic extract pudica officinarum against bacterial clinical isolat minimum inhibitory concentration mic minimal bactericidal concentration mbc test assess their anti-inflammatory potential cytotoxicity vitro qualitative method used ultra-highperformance liquid chromatography coupl high-resolution tandem mass spectrometry uhplc-hrm ms investigate phytochemical properti extract pudica officinarum active against clinical isolat pseudomona aeruginosa ug ml both display anti-inflammatory properti uhplc-hrm ms analysi indicat total 24 phytocompound pudica extract seven officinarum only pudica present acceptable cytotoxicity index further studi antimicrobial potential chromatographic fraction isolat phytomolecul addition data support research safety officinarum context traditional medicine,anti-infective agent botanical extract cell death secondary metabolit,marcu viniciu dias-souza1 mimosa pudica alpinia officinarum largely used different countri treat infectiou diseas well digestive disorder inflammatory condition despite their long history traditional use pharmacological properti both plant poorly describ includ their antimicrobial potential study investigat effectivenes ethanolic extract pudica officinarum against bacterial clinical isolat minimum inhibitory concentration mic minimal bactericidal concentration mbc test assess their anti-inflammatory potential cytotoxicity vitro qualitative method used ultra-highperformance liquid chromatography coupl high-resolution tandem mass spectrometry uhplc-hrm ms investigate phytochemical properti extract pudica officinarum active against clinical isolat pseudomona aeruginosa ug ml both display anti-inflammatory properti uhplc-hrm ms analysi indicat total 24 phytocompound pudica extract seven officinarum only pudica present acceptable cytotoxicity index further studi antimicrobial potential chromatographic fraction isolat phytomolecul addition data support research safety officinarum context traditional medicine anti-infective agent botanical extract cell death secondary metabolit,1.0.0,ca379e01f21222d2
0120-548X,124113_Versión_Final_2026.pdf,124113_Versión_Final_2026_0,es,"flammatory potential and cytotoxicity in vitro. Qualitative methods were used, and ultra-highperformance liquid chromatography coupled to high-resolution tandem mass spectrometry (UHPLC-HRMS/MS) to investigate the","Mimosa pudica y Alpinia officinarum se utilizan ampliamente en distintos países para el tratamiento de enfermedades infecciosas, así como de trastornos digestivos y condiciones inflamatorias. A pesar de su larga historia de uso tradicional, las propiedades farmacológicas de ambas plantas están escasamente descritas, incluido su potencial antimicrobiano. En este estudio se investigó la eficacia de los extractos etanólicos de M. pudica y A. officinarum frente a aislados clínicos bacterianos. Se realizaron ensayos de concentración mínima inhibitoria (CMI) y concentración mínima bactericida (CMB), y evaluamos su potencial antiinflamatorio y citotoxicidad in vitro. Se utilizaron métodos cualitativos y cromatografía líquida de ultra-alto rendimiento acoplada a espectrometría de masas de alta resolución en tándem (UHPLC-HRMS/MS) para investigar las propiedades fitoquímicas de los extractos. M. pudica y A. officinarum mostraron actividad frente a los aislados clínicos de Pseudomonas aeruginosa a 8 µg/mL, y ambas presentaron propiedades antiinflamatorias. El análisis UHPLC-HRMS/MS indicó un total de 24 fitocompuestos en el extracto de M. pudica y siete en el de A. officinarum. Solo M. pudica presentó un índice de citotoxicidad aceptable para futuros estudios sobre el potencial antimicrobiano de fracciones cromatográficas y fitomoléculas aisladas. Además, los datos fundamentan investigaciones sobre la seguridad de A. officinarum en el contexto de la medicina tradicional","Agentes antiinfecciosos, extractos botánicos, metabolitos secundarios, muerte celular. Mimosa pudica and Alpinia officinarum extracts: antimicrobial activity… Acta Biol. Colomb., 31(2) 26-35 Mayo - Agosto 2026 27",marcadores,