0120-9957,57-121-PB.pdf,57-121-PB_1,en,"estancia en la unidad de cuidados intensivos (UCI), estan","Introduction: Acute pancreatitis (AP) is a gastrointestinal condition with a rising global incidence and a significant rate of severe complications. In Colombia, there is a lack of unified epidemiological data, and international prognostic scales lack local validation. To address this, the study describes the clinical charac teristics, management, and outcomes of AP patients at an institution in Barranquilla, providing regional data to optimize clinical management. The principal objective is to describe the sociodemographic, clinical, etio logical characteristics and hospital outcomes of patients with acute pancreatitis (AP) treated at an institution in Barranquilla between January 2020 and December 2024. Methodology: Observational, descriptive study based on the review of 92 medical records of patients older than 18 years who met the diagnostic criteria of the 2012 Atlanta consensus. The study analyzed sociodemographic, etiological, clinical, and paraclinical variables (blood urea nitrogen and hematocrit), findings on contrast-enhanced computed abdominal tomo graphy (Balthazar classification), management, and hospital outcomes. Results: Mean age was 45.2 ± 20 years; 59.8% of patients were women. Low socioeconomic status predominated widely (strata 1 and 2), accounting for 88.0% of cases, followed by middle socioeconomic status (strata 3 and 4) with 12.0%, with no representation from high socioeconomic strata (5 and 6). Biliary etiology was the most frequent (64.5%), followed by idiopathic etiology (20.4%). Mild or moderate forms predominated, with conservative medical management in 93.5% of cases. Complications occurred in 5.8% of patients, and overall mortality was 1.1%. Conclusions: In the analyzed population, acute pancreatitis was mainly of biliary etiology and presented as mild or moderate forms of the disease. The results characterize the regional clinical and epidemiological pro file and describe institutional practice based on a stepwise approach, providing local evidence to strengthen healthcare strategies and guide future validation of prognostic tools in the Colombian context","Acute pancreatitis, biliary lithiasis, Balthazar classification, epidemiological factors. Revista. colomb. Gastroenterol. 2026;41(3):280-288. https://doi.org/10.22516/25007440.1466 282 Trabajo original hospitalarios de los pacientes con diagnóstico de PA aten didos en una institución de la ciudad de Barranquilla, con el propósito de aportar información epidemiológica que sirva como base para un",marcadores,estancia en la unidad de cuidado intensivo uci estan,introduction acute pancreatiti ap gastrointestinal condition rising global incidence significant rate severe complic colombia there lack unifi epidemiological data international prognostic scal lack local validation addres study describ clinical charac teristic management outcom ap patient institution barranquilla provid regional data optimize clinical management principal objective describe sociodemographic clinical etio logical characteristic hospital outcom patient acute pancreatiti ap treat institution barranquilla between january 2020 december 2024 methodology observational descriptive study based review 92 medical record patient older than 18 year who met diagnostic criteria 2012 atlanta consensu study analyz sociodemographic etiological clinical paraclinical variabl blood urea nitrogen hematocrit finding contrast-enhanc comput abdominal tomo graphy balthazar classification management hospital outcom result mean age 45 20 year 59 patient women low socioeconomic statu predominat widely strata account 88 case follow middle socioeconomic statu strata 12 no representation high socioeconomic strata biliary etiology most frequent 64 follow idiopathic etiology 20 mild moderate form predominat conservative medical management 93 case complic occurr patient overall mortality conclusion analyz 