n evaluaron remision clinica necesidad escalamiento terapeutico requerimiento cirugia partir revision histori clinic resultad incluyeron cinco pacient cuatro hombr tres adult dos pediatric todos exposicion previa anti-tnf tres tenian cu dos ec solo paciente pediatrico ec alcanzo remision clinica cuatro no respondieron tres requirieron cambio medicamento tofacitinib vedolizumab dos fueron sometid cirugia no documentaron event advers grav conclusion esta cohorte alta refractaria induccion acelerada mostro baja tasa remision frecuente necesidad escalamiento terapeutico enfermedad inflamatori intestino infliximab colitis ulcerosa enfermedad crohn terapia biologica,1.0.0,a3e56d975271b0ee
0120-9957,57-121-PB.pdf,57-121-PB_4,en,"Se evaluaron la remisión clínica, la necesidad de escalamiento terapéutico y el requerimiento de cirugía","Introduction: Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD), which includes ulcerative colitis (UC) and Crohn’s disease (CD), represents a therapeutic challenge, especially in moderate-to-severe cases and in patients with previous anti-TNF failure. Accelerated infliximab induction, using higher doses or shorter intervals than the standard regimen, has been proposed to achieve faster inflammatory control in patients with a high inflammatory burden or refractory disease. However, evidence in Latin American settings remains limited. The objective of this study was to describe the clinical outcomes of patients with IBD treated with accelerated infliximab induction at a Colombian referral center. Methodology: Observational, descriptive case series that included adult and pediatric patients with UC or CD treated between January 2022 and December 2023 with infliximab 10 mg/kg at weeks 0, 1, and 2. The study evaluated clinical remission, the need for therapeutic escalation, and the requirement for surgery through a review of medical records. Results: The study included five patients (four men; three adults and two pediatric patients), all with previous anti-TNF exposure. Three had UC and two had CD. Only one pediatric patient with CD achieved clinical remission. Four did not respond, three required a change in medication to tofacitinib or vedolizumab, and two underwent surgery. No serious adverse events were documented. Conclusion: In this highly refractory cohort, accelerated induction showed a low remission rate and a frequent need for therapeutic escalation","Inflammatory bowel diseases, infliximab, ulcerative colitis, Crohn’s disease, biological therapy. https://doi.org/10.22516/25007440.1546 Trabajo original Revista. colomb. Gastroenterol. 2026;41(3):309-315. https://doi.org/10.22516/25007440.1546 310 Trabajo original más cortos que el e