0120-9957,57-121-PB.pdf,57-121-PB_6,en,"la hipervigilancia y de factores cognitivos, afectivos y psicosociales en la","Introduction: Esophageal disorders of gut-brain interaction are characterized by symptoms such as heartburn, chest pain, globus sensation, and dysphagia in the absence of structural, inflammatory, motor, or reflux-related abnormalities that adequately account for their occurrence. The Rome V criteria incorporate recent advances in the physiological assessment of the esophagus and should be interpreted in conjunction with the Chicago Classification v4.0 and Lyon Consensus 2.0. Results: This update reviews the five esophageal disorders of gut-brain interaction recognized by Rome V: functional chest pain, functional heartburn, reflux hypersensitivity, globus, and functional dysphagia. Their principal clinical features, diagnostic criteria, pathophysiological mechanisms, and therapeutic strategies are analyzed. Particular emphasis is placed on the central role of esophageal hypersensitivity and central sensory processing mechanisms, as well as on the influence of hypervigilance and cognitive, affective, and psychosocial factors on symptom perception. Conclusions: Rome V proposes an integrated diagnostic approach that allows esophageal symptoms to be interpreted in conjunction with endoscopic and physiological findings, thereby avoiding overinterpretation of minor abnormalities and unnecessary repetition of diagnostic testing. Once relevant structural, inflammatory, motor, and reflux-related diseases have been excluded, management should target the mechanisms perpetuating symptoms, including hypersensitivity and hypervigilance. Patient education, neuromodulation, and psychobehavioral interventions constitute relevant therapeutic tools, although the available evidence supporting many of these interventions remains limited","Gut-brain axis, hypervigilance, dysphagia, Rome V. https://doi.org/10.22516/25007440.1631 Artículo de revisión 325 Trastornos esofági