0120-9957,57-121-PB.pdf,57-121-PB_8,en,é cambia en la categoría trastor,"Introduction: Disorders of gut-brain interaction are common conditions in which gastrointestinal symp toms result from abnormalities in motility, visceral hypersensitivity, mucosal and immune dysfunction, alterations in the microbiota, and impaired central processing. Rome V includes a category in which the contribution of the central nervous system predominates, with pain, rather than luminal dysfunction, defi ning the disorder. Results: Centrally mediated disorders of gastrointestinal pain comprise three entities: centrally mediated abdominal pain syndrome, adult abdominal migraine, and narcotic bowel syndrome. The most far-reaching change concerns the first of these. Under Rome IV, pain was required to be conti nuous and unrelated to food intake, defecation, or menstruation; if it also fluctuated with meals, the patient was excluded from the diagnosis and was often classified as having irritable bowel syndrome. Rome V retains this profile while adding a category in which pain may vary in response to physiological events without ceasing to be continuous. The distinguishing feature is therefore pain continuity rather than its relationship to such events. Conversely, pain associated with irritable bowel syndrome is required to be noncontinuous. Diagnostic criteria, epidemiology, mechanisms, diagnosis, and treatment are reviewed. Conclusions: Centrally mediated disorders of gastrointestinal pain require a shift in clinical reasoning: rather than searching for a luminal lesion, the focus should be placed on treating an identifiable central me chanism. However, the evidence is heterogeneous and derives from small uncontrolled series; therefore, recommendations should be presented accordingly. In Latin America, prevalent organic disease must be excluded, and management should be tailored to the availability of neuromodulators, psychogastroentero logy services, and opioid tapering programs","Abdominal pain; gut-brain axis; hyperalgesia; opioid-related disorders; migraine disorders. Revista. colomb. Gastroenterol. 2026;41(3):334-341. https://doi.org/10.22516/25007440.1636 336 Artículo de revisión 80,1% (IC 95%: 72,4-86,5), frente a sensibilidades de 78,9% (IC 95%: 75,4-82,1) con Roma IV y 87,5% (IC 95%: 84,490,3) con Roma III. El cociente de probabilidad negativo de Roma V fue de 0,4",marcadores,cambia en la categoria trastor,introduction disorder gut-brain interaction common condition which gastrointestinal symp toms result abnormaliti motility visceral hypersensitivity mucosal immune dysfunction alter microbiota impair central process rome includ category which contribution central nervou system predominat pain rather than luminal dysfunction defi ning disorder result centrally mediat disorder gastrointestinal pain comprise three entiti centrally mediat abdominal pain syndrome adult abdominal migraine narcotic bowel syndrome most far-reach change concern first these under rome iv pain requir conti nuou unrelat food intake defecation menstruation if also fluctuat meal patient exclud diagnosi often classifi having irritable bowel syndrome rome retain profile while adding category which pain may vary response physiological event without ceas continuou distinguish feature therefore pain continuity rather than its relationship such event conversely pain associat irritable bowel syndrome requir noncontinuou diagnostic criteria epidemiology mechanism diagnosi treatment review conclusion centrally mediat disorder gastrointestinal pain require shift clinical reason rather than search luminal lesion focu should plac treat identifiable central me chanism however evidence heterogeneou deriv small uncontroll seri therefore recommend should present accord latin america prevalent organic disease must exclud management should tailor availability neuromodulator psychogastroentero logy servic opioid taper program,abdominal pain gut-brain axis hyperalgesia opioid-relat disorder