0120-9957,57-121-PB.pdf,57-121-PB_12,en,"bilidad, en comparación con el adenocarcinoma ductal pan","Introduction: Pancreatic adenosquamous carcinoma is an uncommon exocrine neoplasm that repre sents between 1% and 4% of pancreatic tumors. It is characterized by the presence of glandular and squamous components and is associated with highly aggressive behavior and poor prognosis. Diagnosis usually occurs at advanced stages because of its nonspecific clinical presentation and rapid progression. Case presentation: We present the case of an 82-year-old female patient with abdominal pain, jaundice, and constitutional syndrome. Laboratory studies showed cholestasis, cytolysis, and direct hyperbilirubi nemia. Computed tomography and abdominal magnetic resonance imaging showed a solid-cystic lesion in the tail of the pancreas with involvement of adjacent structures and multiple liver lesions suggestive of metastases. Endoscopic ultrasound with biopsy confirmed the diagnosis of adenosquamous carcinoma through histopathological and immunohistochemical studies (p63 positive in the squamous component and carcinoembryonic antigen positive in the glandular component). The patient had an unfavorable clini cal course and died two months after diagnosis. Discussion: This tumor subtype is associated with a high rate of early dissemination, lymphovascular invasion, and perineural invasion, which limits therapeutic options. Although surgical resection is the treatment of choice in early stages, most cases are unresecta ble at diagnosis. No standardized chemotherapy regimen exists, reflecting the limited available evidence. Conclusion: Pancreatic adenosquamous carcinoma is a rare entity with a poor prognosis, and its timely recognition and histological confirmation are essential. Additional studies are required to optimize its diag nostic and therapeutic approach, consultation, and information at the time of managing this pathology, as it is rare and poorly known","Pancreatic neoplasms,