0123-4471,Virajes+28(1)_5.pdf,Virajes+28(1)_5_0,en,ador posdoctoral,"The Paraguay and Paraná rivers have historically marked the mobility of human beings who inhabited and inhabit both basins. The present research addresses the location, registration and evaluation of the plausible continuity of dugout canoes in the ñeembuqueño’s wetlands (Paraguay), both from the material point of view, as well as in relation to the immaterial knowledge manifested in the traditional navigation and construction techniques traceable today. To this end, the research deploys a transdisciplinary logic that allows for a necessary understanding of the territory, as well as enabling a transition between methodologies that favours the optimisation of all available sources. That is, historical documentation, from the first observations to the present, ethnographic records with canoeists and Ñeembuqueños neighbours, and ethnoarchaeological records of their nautical materiality, both canoes and propulsion elements, which still used today at the threatened wetlands","concept-things, history, indigenous thought, psychogenesis, primitive thought, telluric thought",marcadores,ador posdoctoral,paraguay parana river have historically mark mobility human being who inhabit inhabit both basin present research address location registration evaluation plausible continuity dugout cano neembuqueno wetland paraguay both material point view well relation immaterial knowledge manifest traditional navigation construction techniqu traceable today end research deploy transdisciplinary logic allow necessary understand territory well enabl transition between methodologi favour optimisation all available sourc historical documentation first observ present ethnographic record canoeist neembuqueno neighbour ethnoarchaeological record their nautical materiality both cano propulsion element which still used today threaten wetland,concept-thing history indigenou thought psychogenesi primitive thought telluric thought,ad