1657-9267,um6972857004.pdf,um6972857004_0,en,"Do attachment, emotional","This study examined the relationship between attachment styles, emotional dysregulation, depressive symptoms, and minor mental disorders in a sample of 540 university students (68.33 % women; mean age = 21.6 years) from public and private institutions. Participants completed the Emotional Dysregulation Scale (adult version), the Battery for the Assessment of Depression Indicators, the Brazilian Attachment Scale (adult version), and the Minor Mental Disorders Scale. Confirmatory factor analyses indicated that insecure attachment (preoccupied and fearful) was directly associated with emotional dysregulation, which emerged as a significant mediator in the development of depressive symptoms and minor mental disorders. In contrast, secure attachment showed a protective effect, being associated with positive self-perception and greater emotional regulation capacity. These findings underscore the relevance of early caregiving relationships and highlight emotional dysregulation as a central mechanism linking attachment to depressive symptomatology",depression; minor mental disorders; secure attachment; emotional regulation; university students; mental health,marcadores,do attachment emotional,study examin relationship between attachment styl emotional dysregulation depressive symptom minor mental disorder sample 540 university student 68 33 women mean age 21 year public private institution participant complet emotional dysregulation scale adult version battery assessment depression indicator brazilian attachment scale adult version minor mental disorder scale confirmatory factor analys indicat insecure attachment preoccupi fearful directly associat emotional dysregulation which emerg significant mediator development depressive symptom minor mental disorder contrast secure attachment show protective effect being associat positive self-perception greater emotional regulation capacity these finding unders