1657-9267,um6972857009.pdf,um6972857009_0,en,"comparison, working memory, and fluid","The implementation of new mathematical teaching methodologies, such as the ABN method (Number-Based Algorithm), has shown positive effects on the development of mathematical competencies from Early Childhood Education. General cognitive variables such as working memory and fluid intelligence, together with specific skills such as magnitude comparison, are considered key predictors of mathematical performance. The teaching methodology may influence the relative contribution of these variables to mathematical performance. A comparative study was conducted with 107 early childhood education participants, divided into an ABN group (n = 43) and a non-ABN group (n = 64). General mathematical performance and its cognitive precursors supported by the literature were assessed. Mann–Whitney U tests, bivariate correlations, and linear regressions were conducted. No statistically significant differences were found between the groups. However, regression analyses revealed differences in the variables most strongly associated with mathematical performance depending on the Lydia Mateo-Ramos, Santiago José Reguera-Lozano, Maria del Carmen Canto-López et al. | Universitas Psychologica | V. 25 | January-December | 2026 | 2 teaching methodology, with fluid intelligence showing greater weight in the non-ABN group. These results suggest that different patterns of association between cognitive factors and early mathematical learning may be observed depending on the instructional methodology",early mathematical learning; early childhood education; teaching methodology; ABN,marcadores,comparison work memory fluid,implementation new mathematical teach methodologi such abn method number-bas algorithm has shown positive effect development mathematical competenci early childhood education general cognitive variabl such work memory fluid intelligence together specific skill such magnitude comparison consider k