1657-9267,upsy6972857014.pdf,upsy6972857014_0,en,"How to cite: Barea Vera, A. (2026). RetrievalEnhanced Suggestibility in Forensic Contexts: A","Retrieval practice can paradoxically increase vulnerability to subsequent misinformation, a phenomenon termed retrieval-enhanced suggestibility (RES), with implications for forensic interviewing protocols involving multiple recall attempts. This meta-analysis examines RES in forensicrelevant contexts and identifies boundary conditions moderating the effect. A systematic search identified 26 studies (k = 72 comparisons, N = 4 218) in simulated forensic contexts. Random-effects meta-analyses compared misinformation susceptibility following retrieval practice versus no practice, with moderator analyses (test format, misinformation source, detail type, retention interval, warning). Retrieval practice was associated with increased misinformation acceptance, d = 0.34, 95 % CI [0.23, 0.45], p < 0.001, I² = 56.4 %. Recognition-format tests showed larger RES (d = 0.66) than free recall (d = 0.17); narrative misinformation showed stronger RES (d = 0.48) than decontextualized question-based misinformation (d = -0.19); explicit warnings were associated with no detectable RES (d = -0.07, p = 0.41). In forensicrelevant experimental contexts, initial retrieval practice was associated with greater misinformation vulnerability, whereas free-recall formats, temporal separation, and explicit warnings were associated with reduced or absent effects. These findings inform the evidence base for forensic interview protocols",retrieval-enhanced suggestibility; eyewitness memory; misinformation effect; forensic interviewing; testing effect; memory contamination; investigative protocols,marcadores,how cite barea vera 2026 retrievalenhanc suggestibility forensic context,retrieval practice can paradoxically increase vulnerability subsequent misinformation phenomenon term retrieval-enhanc suggestibility res implic forensic interview protocol involv multiple recall attempt meta-analysi examin res forensicrelevant context identifi boundary condition moderat effect systematic search identifi 26 studi 72 comparison 218 simulat forensic context random-effect meta-analys compar misinformation susceptibility follow retrieval practice versu no practice moderator analys test format misinformation source detail type retention interval warn retrieval practice associat increas misinformation acceptance 34 95 ci 23 45 001 i2 56 recognition-format test show larger res 66 than free recall 17 narrative misinformation show stronger res 48 than decontextualiz question-bas misinformation -0 19 explicit warning associat no detectable res -0 07 41 forensicrelevant experimental context initial retrieval practice associat greater misinformation vulnerability wherea free-recall format temporal separation explicit warning associat reduc absent effect these finding inform evidence base forensic interview protocol,retrieval-enhanc suggestibility eyewitnes memory misinformation effect forensic interview test effect memory contamination investigative protocol,how cite barea vera 2026 retrievalenhanc suggestibility forensic context retrieval practice can paradoxically increase vulnerability subsequent misinformation phenomenon term retrieval-enhanc suggestibility res implic forensic interview protocol involv multiple recall attempt meta-analysi examin res forensicrelevant context identifi boundary condition moderat effect systematic search identifi 26 studi 72 comparison 218 simulat forensic context random-effect meta-analys compar misinformation susceptibility follow retrieval practice versu no practice moderator analys test format misinformation source detail type retention interval warn retrieval practice associat increas misinformation acceptance 34 95 ci 23 45 001 i2 56 recognition-format test show larger res 66 than free recall 17 narrative misinformation show stronger res 48 than decontextualiz question-bas misinformation -0 19 explicit