1657-9267,upsy6972857016.pdf,upsy6972857016_0,en,pero solo tres estudios de validación provinieron de América Latina (todos,"Late-life depression is highly prevalent yet understudied in Latin America, where aging populations are expanding rapidly. This systematic review followed PRISMA 2020 guidelines, searching seven databases (inception to April 2026) for studies examining the Geriatric Depression Scale (GDS) psychometrics or psychosocial intervention efficacy in communitydwelling older adults (≥60 years). Of 1,847 records, 28 studies met criteria (12 psychometric, 16 intervention). The GDS-15 demonstrated adequate internal consistency (α = 0.79 – 0.91) and diagnostic accuracy (sensitivity 0.80 – 0.92; specificity 0.75 – 0.88), but only three validation studies came from Latin America (all Brazil). Psychosocial interventions Gautam Sureshbhai Makwana. | Universitas Psychologica | V. 25 | January-December | 2026 | 2 yielded small-to-moderate effect sizes (Hedges' g = 0.32 – 0.68), yet no randomized controlled trials were conducted in any Latin American country. Optimal GDS cutoffs varied across populations (≥5 Europe, ≥6 Brazil, ≥7 India), suggesting potential differential item functioning. The absence of Spanish-speaking Latin American validation studies and intervention trials represents a critical evidence gap undermining equitable geriatric mental health care. Culturally adapted research co-designed with local stakeholders is urgently needed","geriatric depression; screening tool; psychosocial treatment; Latin America; evidence gap. Late-Life Depression (LLD) represents one of the most pressing public health challenges of the 21st century. As global populations age at unprecedented rates, the prevalence of depressive disorders among older adults has risen correspondingly, with estimates indicating that approximately 13 % to 18 % of comm",marcadores,pero solo tres estudio de validacion provinieron de america latina todo,late-life depression highly prevalent yet 