1692-6013,NOVUM+JUS+20-2+art+10+CURRENT.pdf,NOVUM+JUS+20-2+art+10+CURRENT_0,en,he Territory of Ukraine,"Throughout the 20th century, international humanitarian law (IHL) underwent significant evolution in combating crimes against peace (aggression), crimes against humanity, war crimes, and genocide. The armed aggression of the Russian Federation against Ukraine has led to the temporary occupation of about 20% of the internationally recognized territory of Ukraine (as of 2025), which is accompanied by large-scale violations of human rights and the commission of war crimes. In the context of active hostilities, the absence of legal jurisdiction and the inability of the law enforcement system to function in the occupied territories have created a legal vacuum that complicates the effective investigation of crimes. This poses serious challenges in bringing war criminals to justice, taking into account the norms of IHL, inter-state cooperation, and the use of modern digital forensic technologies. Methodologically, the study employs an interdisciplinary approach that combines content analysis, historical and legal methods, case studies, and legal modeling. Materials and judicial practice of international tribunals, including the ICC, are studied. In this case, particular attention should be paid to the use of artificial intelligence, satellite analytics, 3D modeling, OSINT, and remote collection of digital evidence. Based on international experience and lessons learned from other armed conflicts, the article offers practical recommendations aimed at improving investigation methods and mechanisms for bringing to justice those guilty of committing war crimes. Practical examples of integrating such tools into the investigation process are also considered, in particular, the use of virtual modeling by the Bavarian police. The evolution of IHL in the context of state responsibility, as well as the legal assessment of crimes committed in occupied territories, is also analyzed",war crimes; armed conflict; combatant; genocide; ecocide; occupied territories; digital evidence; artificial intelligence. https://doi.org/10.14718/NovumJus.2026.20.2.10 * Doctor of Law. Candidate of Philosophy. Professor. Government Commissioner for Gender Policy. Email: levchenkokateryna@yahoo.com. ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-8334-7362 ** Doctor of Law. Associate Professor. First Vic,marcadores,he territory ukraine,throughout 20th century international humanitarian law ihl underwent significant evolution combat crim against peace aggression crim against humanity war crim genocide armed aggression russian federation against ukraine has led temporary occupation about 20 internationally recogniz territory ukraine 2025 which accompani large-scale viol human right commission war crim context active hostiliti absence legal jurisdiction inability law enforcement system function occupi territori have creat legal vacuum complicat effective investigation crim pose seriou challeng bring war criminal justice taking into account norm ihl inter-state cooperation use modern digital forensic technologi methodologically study employ interdisciplinary approach combin content analysi historical legal method case studi legal model material judicial practice international tribunal includ icc studi case particular attention should paid use artificial intelligence satellite analytic 3d model osint remote collection digital evidence based international experience lesson learn other armed conflict article offer practical recommend aimed improv investigation method mechanism bring justice those guilty committ war crim practical exampl integrat such tool into investigation proces also consider particular use virtual model bavarian police evolution ihl context state responsibility well legal assessment crim committ occupi territori also analyz,war crim armed conflict combatant genocide ecocide occupi territori digital evidence artificial intelligence http doi org 10 14718 novumju 2026