1692-8415,queratitis.pdf,queratitis_0,en,"spuesta adaptativa, mediada por células T cd8+ y","Herpetic keratitis, predominantly caused by herpes simplex virus type 1 (hsv-1), is one of the leading causes of visual morbidity worldwide. The herpes simplex virus (hsv) has two main serotypes: hsv-1, responsible for ocular pathologies and oral lesions located above the waist, and hsv-2, associated with sexually transmitted diseases, such as recurrent genital lesions, neonatal infections, and increased risk of hiv transmission. hsv-1 infection is a major cause of keratitis, characterized by manifestations such as herpetic dendrites, geographic ulcers, and disciform keratitis, as well as serious complications including blindness. Epidemiologically, hsv-1 affects 67 % of the world’s population before the age of 50, while hsv-2 reaches a prevalence of 11.3 %. Latin America and the Caribbean have a high seroprevalence, with 1 in 5 adults being chronic carriers of hsv-2, reflecting one of the highest rates worldwide. The pathophysiology of keratitis caused by hsv-1 involves viral replication, immune damage to corneal tissue, and recurrent reactivation of the virus, which can be induced by factors such as stress or immunosuppression. This virus first evades the innate immune response, which facilitates its replication in epithelial cells and its latency in the trigeminal ganglion, from where it can reactivate. The adaptive response, mediated by cd8+ and cd4+ T cells, regulates the infection, but viral damage can cause dendritic lesions that progress to stromal ulcers, scarring, neovascularization, and vision loss. Diagnosis requires a detailed medical record, a comprehensive physical examination, and, in complex cases, molecular tests such as polymerase chain reaction. Treatment includes topical and systemic antivirals, such as acyclovir or ganciclovir, and, in specific cases, the controlled use of topical corticosteroids under strict ophthalmological supervision, always accompanied by antiviral therapy",keratitis; her petic keratitis; herpes simplex; viral keratitis; corneal opacity; herpes virus infections; dendritic keratitis,marcadores,spuesta adaptativa mediada por celula cd8,herpetic keratiti predominantly caus herp simplex viru type hsv-1 one lead caus visual morbidity worldwide herp simplex viru hsv has two main serotyp hsv-1 responsible ocular pathologi oral lesion locat above waist hsv-2 associat sexually transmitt diseas such recurrent genital lesion neonatal infection increas risk hiv transmission hsv-1 infection major cause keratiti characteriz manifest such herpetic dendrit geographic ulcer disciform keratiti well seriou complic includ blindnes epidemiologically hsv-1 affect 67 world population before age 50 while hsv-2 reach prevalence 11 latin america caribbean have high seroprevalence adult being chronic carrier hsv-2 reflect one highest rate worldwide pathophysiology keratiti caus hsv-1 involv viral replication immune damage corneal tissue recurrent reactivation viru which can induc factor such stres immunosuppression viru first evad innate immune response which facilitat its replication epithelial cell its latency trigeminal ganglion where can reactivate adaptive response mediat cd8 cd4 cell regulat infection but viral damage can cause dendritic lesion progres stromal ulcer scarr neovascular vision loss diagnosi requir detail medical record comprehensive physical examination complex case molecular test such polymerase chain reaction treatment includ topical systemic antiviral such acyclovir ganciclovir specific case controll use topical corticosteroid under strict ophthalmological supervision alway accompani antiviral therapy,keratiti her petic keratiti herp simplex viral keratiti corneal opacity herp viru infection dendritic keratiti,spuesta adaptativa mediada por celula cd8 herpetic keratiti predominantly caus herp simplex viru type hsv-1 one lead caus visual morbidity worldwide herp simplex viru hsv has two main serot