her precipitation thermal stability particularly alto sinu subregion where vegetation heterogeneity favor greater availability host plant contrast more homogeneou environment such those coastal subregion limit speci richnes reduc variety plant form conclud distribution cassidinae link availability specific food resourc these phytophagou insect which exhibit high host specificity coleoptera habitat heterogeneity multivariate ordination neotropical region seasonality composicion taxonomica ecologia de las especi de cassidinae en cordoba acta biol colomb 31 36-48 mayo agosto 2026 37,1.0.0,98e9a3479a7f07ce
1900-1649,124113_Versión_Final_2026.pdf,124113_Versión_Final_2026_0,en,", Marcus Vinícius Dias-Souza1*","Mimosa pudica and Alpinia officinarum are largely used in different countries to treat infectious diseases as well as digestive disorders and inflammatory conditions. Despite their long history of traditional use, the pharmacological properties of both plants are poorly described, including their antimicrobial potential. This study investigated the effectiveness of the ethanolic extracts of M. pudica and A. officinarum against bacterial clinical isolates. The minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC) and minimal bactericidal concentration (MBC) tests and assessed their anti-inflammatory potential and cytotoxicity in vitro. Qualitative methods were used, and ultra-highperformance liquid chromatography coupled to high-resolution tandem mass spectrometry (UHPLC-HRMS/MS) to investigate the phytochemical properties of the extracts. M. pudica and A. officinarum were active against the clinical isolates of Pseudomonas aeruginosa at 8 µg/mL, and both displayed anti-inflammatory properties. UHPLC-HRMS/MS analysis indicated a total of 24 phytocompounds in M. pudica extract and seven in A. officinarum. Only M. pudica presented an acceptable cytotoxicity index for further studies on the antimicrobial potential of chromatographic fractions and isolated phytomolecules. In addition, the data supports research on the safety of A. officinarum in the context of traditional medicine","Anti-infective agents, botanical extracts, cell death, secondary metabolites",marcadores,marcu viniciu dias-souza1,mimosa pudica alpinia officinarum largely used different countri treat infectiou diseas well digestive disorder inflammatory condition despite their long history traditional use pharmacological properti both plant poorly describ includ their antimicrobial potential study investigat effectivenes ethanolic extract pudica officinarum against bacterial clinical isolat minimum inhibitory concentration mic minimal bactericidal concentration mbc test assess their anti-inflammatory potential cytotoxicity vitro qualitative method used ultra-highperformance liquid chromatography coupl high-resolution tandem mass spectrometry uhplc-hrm ms investigate phytochemical properti extract pudica officinarum active against clinical isolat pseudomona aeruginosa ug ml both display anti-inflammatory properti uhplc-hrm ms analysi indicat total 24 phytocompound pudica extract seven officinarum only pudica present acceptable cytotoxicity index further studi antimicrobial potential chromatographic fraction isolat phytomolecul addition data support research safety officinarum context traditional medicine,anti-infective agent botanical extract cell death secondary metabolit,marcu viniciu dias-souza1 mimosa pudica alpinia officinarum largely used different countri treat infectiou diseas well digestive disorder inflammatory condition despite their long history traditional use pharmacological properti both plant poorly describ includ their antimicrobial potential study investigat effectivenes ethanolic extract pudica officinarum against bacterial clinical isolat minimum inhibitory concentration mic minimal bactericidal concentration mbc test assess their anti-inflammatory potential cytotoxicity vitro qualitative method used ultra-highperformance liquid chromatography coupl high-resolution tandem mass spectrometry uhplc-hrm ms investigate phytochemical properti extract pudica officinarum active against clinical isolat pseudomona aeruginosa ug ml both display anti-inflammatory properti uhplc-hrm ms analysi indicat total 24 phytocompound pudica extract seven officinarum only pudica present acceptable cytotoxicity index further studi antimicrobial potential chromatographic fraction isolat phytomolecul addition data support research safety officinarum context traditional medicine anti-infective agent botanical extract cell death secondary metabolit,1.0.0,bb67e41ce2c77bbc
1900-1649,124113_Versión_Final_2026.pdf,124113_Versión_Final_2026_0,es,"flammatory potential and cytotoxicity in vitro. Qualitative methods were used, and ultra-highperformance liquid chromatography coupled to high-resolution tandem mass spectrometry (UHPLC-HRMS/MS) to investigate the","Mimosa pudica y Alpinia officinarum se utilizan ampliamente en distintos países para el tratamiento de enfermedades infecciosas, así como de trastornos digestivos y condiciones inflamatorias. A pesar de su larga historia de uso tradicional, las propiedades farmacológicas de ambas plantas están escasamente descritas, incluido su potencial antimicrobiano. En este estudio se investigó la eficacia de los extractos etanólicos de M. pudica y A. officinarum frente a aislados clínicos bacterianos. Se realizaron ensayos de concentración mínima inhibitoria (CMI) y concentración mínima bactericida (CMB), y evaluamos su potencial antiinflamatorio y citotoxicidad in vitro. Se utilizaron métodos cualitativos y cromatografía líquida de ultra-alto rendimiento acoplada a espectrometría de masas de alta resolución en tándem (UHPLC-HRMS/MS) para investigar las propiedades fitoquímicas de los extractos. M. pudica y A. officinarum mostraron actividad frente a los aislados clínicos de Pseudomonas aeruginosa a 8 µg/mL, y ambas presentaron propiedades antiinflamatorias. El análisis UHPLC-HRMS/MS indicó un total de 24 fitocompuestos en el extracto de M. pudica y siete en el de A. officinarum. Solo M. pudica presentó un índice de citotoxicidad aceptable para futuros estudios sobre el potencial antimicrobiano de fracciones cromatográficas y fitomoléculas aisladas. Además, los datos fundamentan investigaciones sobre la seguridad de A. officinarum en el contexto de la medicina tradicional","Agentes antiinfecciosos, extractos botánicos, metabolitos secundarios, muerte celular. Mimosa pudica and Alpinia officinarum extracts: antimicrobial activity… Acta Biol. Colomb., 31(2) 26-35 Mayo - Agosto 2026 27",marcadores,