1909-7700,08_MED+34(1)_8498+(1).pdf,08_MED+34(1)_8498+(1)_0,en,"percepción del peso se asociaron a la mayor prevalencia de obesidad, y esta fue menor en quienes","Objective: To identify the relationship between socio-labor conditions, dietary habits, and obesity among older workers (≥ 60 years old) engaged in subsistence work in Medellín, Colombia, 2021. Materials and Methods: A cross-sectional study was conducted using primary data sources, drawing from a macro-study of 656 informal workers selected via snowball sampling. A subsample of 208 participants aged ≥ 60 was included for this analysis. Variables: Obesity, classified according to body mass index (BMI); sociodemographic and labor conditions; dietary habits; and comorbidities. Univariate, bivariate, and multivariate analyses were performed. Results: Of the participants, 68.7% were men, 72.6% were aged 60–69, 73.1% were heads of household, 54.8% had spent over 30 years in their line of work, 72.1% were active/very active, and 58.7% ate three times a day. Consumption of fats/oils, tubers/plantains, and cereals exceeded 5 times per week; nonetheless, 60.6% perceived their weight as appropriate. Comorbidities included hypertension (72.3%) and diabetes (24.6%). The prevalence of obesity was 17.3%, and it was significantly higher (𝑝 < 0.05) among women (PR = 2.20; CI: 1.22–3.94), individuals aged 60–69 (PR = 4.15; CI: 1.32–13.00), and those who perceived themselves as overweight—a condition that contributed the most to explaining the higher prevalence (aPR = 13.68; CI: 5.29–35.36). Conclusions: Age, sex, and weight perception were associated with a higher prevalence of obesity, which was lower among those who engaged in physical activity. A dietary pattern rich in high-calorie foods and low in fruit consumption was highlighted within a hostile work environment. This study provides evidence that could be useful for designing and implementing accessible food education programs and the early recognition of excess weight in areas close to workplaces",obesity; older adults; informal workers; COVID-19; dietary habits. Obesidade e trabalho de subsistência em pessoas idosas,marcadores,percepcion del peso se asociaron la mayor prevalencia de obesidad esta fue menor en quien,objective identify relationship between socio-labor condition dietary habit obesity among older worker 60 year old engag subsistence work medellin colombia 2021 material method cross-sectional study conduct using primary data sourc draw macro-study 656 informal worker select via snowball sampl subsample 208 participant aged 60 includ analysi variabl obesity classifi accord body mass index bmi sociodemographic labor condition dietary habit comorbiditi univariate bivariate multivariate analys perform result participant 68 men 72 aged 60-69 73 head household 54 had spent over 30 year their line work 72 active very active 58 ate three time day consumption fats oils tuber plantain cereal exceed time per week nonetheles 60 perceiv their weight appropriate comorbiditi includ hypertension 72 diabet 24 prevalence obesity 17 significantly higher 05 among women pr 20 ci 22-3 94 individual aged 60-69 pr 15 ci 32-13 00 those who perceiv themselv overweight--a condition contribut most explain higher prevalence apr 13 68 ci 29-35 36 conclusion age sex weight perception associat higher prevalence obesity which lower among those who engag physical activity dietary pattern rich high-calorie food low fruit consumption highlight within hostile work environment study provid evidence could useful design implement accessible food education program early recognition exces weight area close workplac,obesity older adult informal worker covid-19 dietary habit obesidade trabalho de subsistencia em pessoa idosa,percepcion del peso se asociaron la mayor prevalencia de obesidad esta fue menor en quien objective identify relationship between socio-labor condition dietary habit obesity among older worker 60 year old engag subsistence work medellin