1909-7719,7746_RFCE+34(1)_3.pdf,7746_RFCE+34(1)_3_0,en,Bankruptcy Prediction and,"This study uses a meta-analytic approach based on random-effects models to examine the effectiveness of Machine Learning (ML) techniques in predicting business bankruptcies. Sixty-four studies were analyzed and categorized by two key performance metrics: Area Under the Curve (AUC) and Accuracy (ACC). The three main findings are: (1) While absolute performance differences appear small, significant variability exists across ML models depending on the context; (2) no statistically significant differences were found between the predictive capabilities of AUC and ACC in classifying firms as bankrupt or non-bankrupt; and (3) substantial heterogeneity among studies was confirmed, driven by firm characteristics, industry, and methodological differences. These results emphasize the importance of customizing ML models to specific datasets and highlight how contextual factors shape predictive accuracy. This study contributes to the literature by addressing the underexplored role of firm heterogeneity and offering actionable insights for practitioners, policymakers, and researchers aiming to improve financial risk prediction","business bankruptcy; meta-analysis; machine learning; heterogeneity. JEL classification: G33, L25, M15, M21. * Research article. a Master in Finance. Research professor, Universidad Externado de Colombia (Bogotá, Colombia). Email: yuly.franco1@uexternado.edu.co; ORCID: https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2938-9331 b Master in Economics. Part-time Professor, Universidad Piloto de Colombia (Bogotá, Colo",marcadores,bankruptcy prediction,study uses meta-analytic approach based random-effect model examine effectivenes machine learn ml techniqu predict busines bankruptci sixty-four studi analyz categoriz two key performance metric area under curve auc accuracy acc three main finding while absolute performance differenc appear small significant variability exist acros ml model depend context no statistically significant differenc found between predictive capabiliti auc acc classify firm bankrupt non-bankrupt substantial heterogeneity among studi confirm driven firm characteristic industry methodological differenc these result emphasize importance customiz ml model specific dataset highlight how contextual factor shape predictive accuracy study contribut literature address underexplor role firm heterogeneity offer actionable insight practitioner policymaker researcher aiming improve financial risk prediction,busines bankruptcy meta-analysi machine learn heterogeneity jel classification g33 l25 m15 m21 research article master finance research professor universidad externado de colombia bogota colombia email yuly franco1 uexternado edu co orcid http orcid org 0000-0003-2938-9331 master economic part-time professor universidad piloto de colombia bogota colo,bankruptcy prediction study uses meta-analytic approach based random-effect model examine effectivenes machine learn ml techniqu predict busines bankruptci sixty-four studi analyz categoriz two key performance metric area under curve auc accuracy acc three main finding while absolute performance differenc appear small significant variability exist acros ml model depend context no statistically significant differenc found between predictive capabiliti auc acc classify firm bankrupt non-bankrupt substantial heterogeneity among studi confirm driven firm characteristic industry methodological differenc these result emphasize importance customiz ml model specific dataset highlight how contextual factor shape predictive accuracy study contribut literature address underexplor role firm heterogeneity offer actionable insight practitioner policymaker researcher aiming improve financial risk prediction busines bankruptcy meta-analysi machine learn heterogeneity jel classification g33 l25 m15 m21 research article master finance research professor universidad externado de colombia bogota colombia email yuly fran