2011-8643,"cangulomuoz,+Gestor_a+de+la+revista,+Magistro+Vol.+8+No.+16-Hermes.pdf","cangulomuoz,+Gestor_a+de+la+revista,+Magistro+Vol.+8+No.+16-Hermes_0",en,"nto Educativo y Comunicación, de Rudecolombia, CADE Universidad Tecnológica de Pereira. Magíster en Historia de la Universidad","In this essay I propose to show the metaphor as an essential element in the writing of renascent indigenous people, through which they express the world dissimilar and different from the discourses taught in western culture. The title itself is a metaphor in which I show how Hermes the messenger of the Greek gods, and Sac Pakal the Mayan messenger dove, find crossed times and destinations so their messages fail to meet prevailing until today, especially in academic circles, the discourse of philosophy and sciences in which time is linear and constructions are based on universal concepts (or better yet universalized by the West) from which the word is written and spoken, disqualifying logics and other worldviews, inherent to the nations dominated by colonization. I start from the reading of Lakoff and Johnson: Metaphors in everyday life, in which an interpretation is made of metaphorical language, not only as a literary figure “for the rhetorical embellishment, but as part of everyday language that Hermes y Sacbakal mensajeros cruzados 161 m ISSN: 2011-8643, Magistro, vol. 8, n.º 16, julio-diciembre de 2014, pp. 159-177 affects the form in which we perceive, think and act”. I apply the findings to the interpretation of one of the works by Jorge Miguel Cocom Pech, from a hermeneutic perspective","Language, metaphor, diversity, interculturality, postmetaphysical philosophy Hermes y Sackabal messagers croisés Résumé Dans cet essai j’ai l’intention de montrer la métaphore comme élément essentiel dans l’écriture des peuples indigènes renaissant, à travers laquelle ils expriment le monde d’une autre façon et différent au monde enseigné dans les discours de la culture occidentale. Le t",marca