2027-1034,851-239-PB.pdf,851-239-PB_16,en,ogido bajo el título “Entre,"This chronicle presents a pedagogical experience carried out in Natural Sciences with secondary school students, focused on the analysis of different positions on the origin of human beings. Through a spontaneous but structured debate, scientific views (such as Darwin–Wallace’s theory of evolution and Oparin’s abiogenesis), traditional religious positions (such as the creationist view based on Genesis), as well as conciliatory and even skeptical perspectives were addressed. The first stage of the debate, titled “Crossroads of Positions: Science, Faith, and Other Possibilities”, allowed students to identify the foundations of each position and to practice argumentation from different perspectives. In the second stage, “Why Didn’t Monkeys Evolve? A Popular Doubt Dismantled by Science”, common beliefs were deconstructed through scientific evidence, promoting epistemological literacy. From the subsequent analysis, compiled under the title “Between Darwin and Genesis: What the Classroom Revealed to Us”, the potential of the classroom as a space for dialogue between ways of knowing, respect for diverse beliefs, and the promotion of critical thinking was acknowledged. Finally, in “From Debate to Practice: Lessons for the Teacher”, the text reflected on the pedagogical challenges involved in teaching biology in contexts where science, faith, and culture converge, advocating for a rigorous, inclusive teaching approach that is sensitive to the plurality of worldviews",evolution; student; debate; science; faith,marcadores,ogido bajo el titulo entre,chronicle present pedagogical experience carri out natural scienc secondary school student focus analysi different position origin human being through spontaneou but structur debate scientific view such darwin-wallace theory evolution oparin abiogenesi traditional religiou position such creationist view based genesi well conciliatory even skeptical perspectiv address first stage debate titl crossroad position science faith other possibiliti allow student identify found each position practice argumentation different perspectiv second stage why didn monkey evolve popular doubt dismantl science common belief deconstruct through scientific evidence promot epistemological literacy subsequent analysi compil under title between darwin genesi what classroom reveal us potential classroom space dialogue between ways know respect diverse belief promotion critical think acknowledg finally debate practice lesson teacher text reflect pedagogical challeng involv teach biology context where science faith culture converge advocat rigorou inclusive teach approach sensitive plurality worldview,evolution student debate science faith,ogido bajo el titulo entre chronicle present pedagogical experience carri out natural scienc secondary school student focus analysi different position origin human being through spontaneou but structur debate scientific view such darwin-wallace theory evolution oparin abiogenesi traditional religiou position such creationist view based genesi well conciliatory even skeptical perspectiv address first stage debate titl crossroad position science faith other possibiliti allow student identify found each position practice argumentation different perspectiv second stage why didn monkey evolve popular doubt dismantl science common belief deconstruct through scientific evidence promot epistemological literacy subsequent analysi compil under title between darwin genesi what classroom reveal us potential classroom space dialogue between ways know respect diverse belief promotion critical think acknowledg finally debate practice lesson teacher text reflect pedagogical challeng involv teach biology context where science faith culture converge advocat rigorou inclusive teach approach sensitive plurality worldview evolution student debate science faith,1.0.0,5e61fe9599be854c
2027-1352,Art-890_Ajem.pdf,Art-890_Ajem_0,en,ORIGINAL ARTICLE,"Colombia is the second country with the highest number of Amerindian ethnic groups, with autonomy and self-governance, allowing them to preserve their culture and Amerindian genetic heritage. In some of these communities, studies using markers such as mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA), the non-recombining region of the Y chromosome (NRY), and Ancestry Informative Markers (AIMs) have helped clarify the settlement processes and genetic admixture of these populations. In this study, we analyzed genome-wide Single Nucleotide Polymorphism (SNP) data from four indigenous populations of Colombia, the Wayuu, Nasa-Páez, Pijao, and Embera-Kátio, genotyped on the Axiom SpainBA panel. We were interested in determining the genetic ancestry and structure, as well as patterns of genetic variation in these populations. We collected personal questionnaires to determine family relationships and included only unrelated individuals for further analyses. Population genetic analyses, including other Native and non-Indigenous American populations, showed genetic affinities to other Indigenous American populations and varied levels of genetic admixture with non-American sources. Furthermore, our analyses suggest that distinct demographic histories, including isolation, genetic drift associated with reduced effective population size (Ne), and admixture, have shaped patterns of genetic variation and population structure among Colombian Indigenous populations","Ancestry; Colombian ethnic groups; Population structure; Array, Variability; Genetic dynamics. 1",marcadores,original article,colombia second country highest number amerindian ethnic group autonomy self-governance allow them preserve their culture amerindian genetic heritage some these communiti studi using marker such mitochondrial dna mtdna non-recombin region chromosome nry ancestry informative marker aims have help clarify settlement process genetic admixture these popul study we analyz genome-wide single nucleotide polymorphism snp data four indigenou popul colombia wayuu nasa-paez pijao embera-katio genotyp axiom spainba panel we interest determin genetic ancestry structure well pattern genetic variation these popul we collect personal questionnair determine family relationship includ only unrelat individual further analys population genetic analys includ other native non-indigenou american popul show genetic affiniti other indigenou american popul vari level genetic admixture non-american sourc furthermore our analys suggest distinct demographic histori includ isolation genetic drift associat reduc effective population size ne admixture have shap pattern genetic variation population structure among colombian indigenou popul,ancestry colombian ethnic group population structure array variability genetic dynamic,original article colombia second country highest number amerindian ethnic group autonomy self-governance allow them preserve their culture amerindian genetic heritage some these communiti studi using marker such mitochondrial dna mtdna non-recombin region chromosome nry ancestry informative marker aims have help clarify settlement process genetic admixture these popul study we analyz genome-wide single nucleotide polymorphism snp data four indigenou popul colombia wayuu nasa-paez pijao embera-katio genotyp axiom spainba panel we interest determin genetic ancestry structure well pattern genetic variation these popul we collect personal questionnair determine family relationship includ only unrelat individual further analys population genetic analys includ other native non-indigenou american popul show genetic affiniti other indigenou american popul vari level genetic admixture non-american sourc furthermore our analys suggest distinct demographic histori includ isolation genetic drift associat reduc effective population size ne admixture have shap pattern genetic variation population structure among colombian indigenou popul ancest