2027-5366,Díkaion35(1)_26733-Potestades_7_PDF_Vera+Sánchez;Navarro+Schiappacasse.pdf,Díkaion35(1)_26733-Potestades_7_PDF_Vera+Sánchez;Navarro+Schiappacasse_0,es,o autoincriminación. Las reglas que regulan la prueba ilícita y las que dan atribuciones a,"3 Díkaion - eISSN 2027-5366, Año 40 - Vol. 35 Núm. 1 - Chía, Colombia - 2026 / e3517 Juan Sebastián Vera Sánchez; María Pilar Navarro Schiappacasse Abstract The use of evidence collected during a tax case procedure in a subsequent tax criminal proceeding violates the privilege against self-incrimination. The rules governing unlawfully-obtained evidence and those granting powers to the Tax Authority are power-conferring laws. In this case, the purpose is a condition of the enabling act, but tax purposes are different from criminal purposes. If the purpose of enabling is not fulfilled, the institutional result (i.e., lawfully-obtained evidence) does not occur. Furthermore, this case constitutes a violation of the privilege against self-incrimination because the accused’s cooperation is no longer voluntary, as tax law establishes duties of cooperation and, in some cases, penalties for failing to provide information","Unlawfully-obtained evidence; tax examination; tax crime; privilege against self-incrimination; rules of jurisdiction. 4 Díkaion - eISSN 2027-5366 / e3517 Potestades de la administración tributaria, derecho de no autoincriminación y prueba ilícita",marcadores,autoincriminacion regl regulan prueba ilicita dan atribucion,dikaion eissn 2027-5366 ano 40 vol 35 num chia colombia 2026 e3517 juan sebastian vera sanchez maria pilar navarro schiappacasse abstract the use of evidence collected during tax case procedure in subsequent tax criminal proceeding violat the privilege against self-incrimination the rules governing unlawfully-obtained evidence and those granting powers to the tax authority are power-conferring laws in this case the purpose is condition of the enabling act but tax purpos are different from crim