2027-5366,Díkaion35(1)_26733-Potestades_7_PDF_Vera+Sánchez;Navarro+Schiappacasse.pdf,Díkaion35(1)_26733-Potestades_7_PDF_Vera+Sánchez;Navarro+Schiappacasse_0,en,"n este caso, la","The use of evidence collected during a tax case procedure in a subsequent tax criminal proceeding violates the privilege against self-incrimination. The rules governing unlawfully-obtained evidence and those granting powers to the Tax Authority are power-conferring laws. In this case, the purpose is a condition of the enabling act, but tax purposes are different from criminal purposes. If the purpose of enabling is not fulfilled, the institutional result (i.e., lawfully-obtained evidence) does not occur. Furthermore, this case constitutes a violation of the privilege against self-incrimination because the accused’s cooperation is no longer voluntary, as tax law establishes duties of cooperation and, in some cases, penalties for failing to provide information","Unlawfully-obtained evidence; tax examination; tax crime; privilege against self-incrimination; rules of jurisdiction. 4 Díkaion - eISSN 2027-5366 / e3517 Potestades de la administración tributaria, derecho de no autoincriminación y prueba ilícita",marcadores,este caso la,use evidence collect during tax case procedure subsequent tax criminal proceed violat privilege against self-incrimination rule govern unlawfully-obtain evidence those grant power tax authority power-conferr laws case purpose condition enabl act but tax purpos different criminal purpos if purpose enabl not fulfill institutional result lawfully-obtain evidence does not occur furthermore case constitut violation privilege against self-incrimination because accus cooperation no longer voluntary tax law establish duti cooperation some case penalti fail provide information,unlawfully-obtain evidence tax examination tax crime privilege against self-incrimination rule jurisdiction dikaion eissn 2027-5366 e3517 potestad de la administracion tributaria derecho de no autoincr