2145-2660,Vitae+33-1++363490+(1).pdf,Vitae+33-1++363490+(1)_0,en,"Researcher, Universidad Nacional","Background: Patients are exposed to medication errors throughout the health care system. The automation of drug distribution processes has been proposed as an alternative to reduce errors. Automated Distribution Equipment (ADE) has advantages, but its impact on safety remains inconclusive. This study seeks to evaluate the safety of the use of medications dispensed through ADE in hospital services in Colombia. Methodology: Descriptive and evaluative mixed study. Interviews were conducted with pharmaceutical chemists responsible for the pharmaceutical services of hospitals with ADE equipment installed. The “Questionnaire for the Evaluation of the Safety of Automated Drug Dispensing Systems” of the Institute for the Safe Use of Medicines (ISMP) of Spain was followed. The data were independently analyzed and categorized by two researchers. The percentage of compliance, associated risks, and safety barriers pertinent to the use of the equipment were identified. The latter two were classified according to the processes they were associated with, either around the use of the equipment or specific to its use. Results: The three institutions analyzed achieved 81.2% compliance with the aspects evaluated by the ISMP questionnaire. However, opportunities for improvement were evident. Regarding the processes around the use of ADEs, a risk of violation of restricted access to products was observed, alongside a lack of pharmaceutical validation of prescriptions. Regarding the processes for the use of ADEs, information provided by the equipment at the time of distribution is limited, and there is a risk of confusion due to storage limitations.Conclusions: The limited number of Colombian health institutions that have implemented ADE cover main operational processes, including the integration of medical prescriptions and standardized daily procedures. The assessment of safety in use is acceptable, but further enhancements, particularly pharmaceutical validation of prescriptions, are needed. Future research is needed in the local context to evaluate the efficiency versus costs and incidence of medication errors","Automation, Pharmacy; Automatic Distribution Equipment; Patient Safety; Medication Errors",marcadores,researcher universidad nacional,background patient expos medication error throughout health care system automation drug distribution process has been propos alternative reduce error automat distribution equipment ade has advantag but its impact safety remain inconclusive study seek evaluate safety use medic dispens through ade hospital servic colombia methodology descriptive evaluative mixed study interview conduct pharmaceutical chemist responsible pharmaceutical servic hospital ade equipment install questionnaire evaluation safety automat drug dispens system institute safe use medicin ismp spain follow data independently analyz categoriz two researcher percentage compliance associat risk safety barrier pertinent use equipment identifi latter two classifi accord process they associat either around use equipment specific its use result three institution analyz achiev 81 compliance aspect evaluat ismp questionnaire however opportuniti improvement evident regard process around use ades risk violation restrict acces product observ alongside lack pharmaceutical validation prescription regard process use ades information provid equipment time distribution limit there risk confusion due storage limit conclusion limit number colombian health institution have implement ade cover main operational process includ integration medical prescription standardiz daily procedur assessment safety use acceptable but further enhancement particularly pharmaceutical validation prescription need future research need local context evaluate efficiency versu cost incidence medication error,automation pharmacy automatic distribution equipment patient safety medication error,researcher universidad nacional background patient expos medication error throughout health care system automation drug distribution process has been propos alternative reduce error automat distribution equipment ade has advantag but its impact safety remain inconclusive study seek evaluate safety use medic dispens through ade hospital servic colombia methodology descriptive evaluative mixed study interview conduct pharmaceutical chemist responsible pharmaceutical servic hospital ade equipment install questionnaire evaluation safety automat drug dispens system institute safe use medicin ismp spain follow data independently analyz categoriz two researcher percentage compliance associat risk safety barrier pertinent use equipment identifi latter two classifi accord process they associat either around use equipment specific its use result three institution analyz achiev 81 compliance aspect evaluat ismp questionnaire however opportuniti improvement evident regard process around use ades risk violation restrict acces product observ alongside lack pharmaceutical validation prescription regard process use ades information provid equipment time distribution limit there risk confusion due storage limit conclusion limit number colombian health institution have implement ade cover main operational process includ integration medical prescription standardiz daily procedur assessment safety use acceptable but further enhancement particularly pharmaceutical validation prescription need future research need local context evaluate efficiency versu cost incidence medication error automation pharmacy automatic distribution equipment patient safety medication error,1.0.0,069e08996ed89a00
2145-2660,Vitae+33-1++363870.pdf,Vitae+33-1++363870_0,en,"How to cite: Jiménez-Toro, I., Hincapie,","BACKGROUND: A rapid and cost-effective HPLC-UV method was developed and validated for the quantification of acetaminophen in human plasma. OBJECTIVE: To develop and validate a simple and rapid assay for acetaminophen quantification in human plasma, aimed at strengthening clinical monitoring, particularly in overdose scenarios. METHODS: The method utilizes isocratic elution with ultraviolet detection and a simple one-step protein precipitation using acetonitrile. This approach yields a short run time of approximately 8 minutes and requires a minimal injection volume of 20 μL. The assay provides a broad dynamic range (2–250 μg/mL), covering both therapeutic and toxic concentrations. RESULTS: The calibration curve exhibited excellent linearity (R² = 0.999), and method validation demonstrated acceptable precision (CV ≤ 15% for quality control samples and ≤ 20% at the lower limit of quantification) and accuracy (within ±15%, or ±20% at the LLOQ). Its clinical applicability was confirmed by successfully quantifying acetaminophen in 55 plasma samples from healthy volunteers receiving a therapeutic oral dose of 1000 mg. Although no internal standard was used, the method’s rigorous validation and minimal matrix effects support its omission, in accordance with FDA, EMA, and ICH guidelines. CONCLUSION: This method offers a reliable and accessible alternative to more complex analytical techniques, with significant potential to support patient care in both therapeutic drug monitoring and overdose management. Further validation in overdose settings is required and will be pursued as part of future work","Acetaminophen; HPLC; Human Plasma; Therapeutic Drug Monitoring, Overdose",marcadores,how cite jimenez-toro hincapie,background rapid cost-effective hplc-uv method develop validat quantification acetaminophen human plasma objective develop validate simple rapid assay acetaminophen 