2145-2660,Vitae+33-2++363745+(1).pdf,Vitae+33-2++363745+(1)_0,en,"), Al-Azhar University,","Background: Ononis vaginalis belongs to the Fabaceae family and is distributed in the Mediterranean region. Objectives: This study focuses on the phytochemical elucidation, anticancer, and antimicrobial activities of O. vaginalis. Methods: The phytochemical profile was characterized by LC-ESI-MS/MS. The MTT assay and disk diffusion technique were used to investigate the anticancer and antimicrobial properties, respectively. Results: 3,5,7-trihydroxy4’-methoxy flavone, apigenin, formononetin, daidzein, and hesperidin were the major identified anticancer and antimicrobial constituents in methanolic O. vaginalis extract. The plant showed moderate anticancer effects against breast cancer (MCF7) (IC50 = 46.3 ± 5.2 µg/mL), human lung adenocarcinomas (A549) (IC50 = 56.7 ± 8.8 µg/mL), glioma cell lines (U87) (IC50 = 95.5 ± 10.3 µg/mL) and colorectal adenocarcinoma (DLD-1) (IC50 = 76.7 ± 0.2µg/mL), relative to cisplatin. However, selectivity indices were below 3 for all tested cancer cell lines, indicating limited selectivity toward cancer cells relative to normal human dermal fibroblasts. It also showed antibacterial activities against five pathogenic strains, four Escherichia coli spp., and Proteus mirabilis. Conclusion: O. vaginalis contained constituents that may be responsible for moderate anticancer and antimicrobial activities. Nevertheless, the low selectivity indices observed for the crude extract indicate limited cancer-cell specificity, highlighting the need for further fractionation, isolation, and characterization of the active constituents before their therapeutic potential can be fully assessed","O. vaginalis, phytochemicals, antimicrobial, anticancer, LC-ESI-MS/MS. Antecedentes: Ononis vaginalis pertenece a la familia Fabaceae y se distribuye en la región mediterránea. Objetivos: Este estudio se centró en la elucidación fitoquímica y en la evaluación de las actividades anticancerígena y antimicrobiana de O. vaginalis. Métodos: El perfil fitoquímico se caracterizó mediante cromatografí",marcadores,al-azhar university,background ononi vaginali belong fabaceae family distribut mediterranean region objectiv study focus phytochemical elucidation anticancer antimicrobial activiti vaginali method phytochemical profile characteriz lc-esi-m ms mtt assay disk diffusion technique used investigate anticancer antimicrobial properti respectively result 7-trihydroxy4 -methoxy flavone apigenin formononetin daidzein hesperidin major identifi anticancer antimicrobial constituent methanolic vaginali extract plant show moderate anticancer effect against breast cancer mcf7 ic50 46 ug ml human lung adenocarcinoma a549 ic50 56 ug ml glioma cell line u87 ic50 95 10 ug ml colorectal adenocarcinoma dld-1 ic50 76 2ug ml relative cisplatin however selectivity indic below all test cancer cell line indicat limit selectivity toward cancer cell relative normal human dermal fibroblast also show antibacterial activiti against five pathogenic strain four escherichia coli spp proteu mirabili conclusion vaginali contain constituent may responsible moderate anticancer antimicrobial activiti nevertheles low selectivity indic observ crude extract indicate limit cancer-cell specificity highlight need further fractionation isolation character active constituent before their therapeutic potential can fully assess,vaginali phytochemical antimicrobial anticancer lc-esi-m ms antecedent ononi vaginali pertenece la familia fabaceae se distribuye en la region mediterranea objetivo este estudio se centro en la elucidacion fitoquimica en la evaluacion de las actividad anticancerigena antimicrobiana de vaginali metodo el perfil fitoquimico se caracterizo mediante cromatografi,al-azhar university background ononi vaginali belong fabaceae family distribut mediterranean region objectiv study focus phytochemical elucidation anticancer antimicrobial activiti vaginali method phytochemical profile characteriz lc-esi-m ms mtt assay disk diffusion technique used investigate anticancer antimicrobial properti respectively result 7-trihydroxy4 -methoxy flavone apigenin formononetin daidzein hesperidin major identifi anticancer antimicrobial constituent methanolic vaginali extract plant show moderate anticancer effect against breast cancer mcf7 ic50 46 ug ml human lung adenocarcinoma a549 ic50 56 ug ml glioma cell line u87 ic50 95 10 ug ml colorectal adenocarcinoma dld-1 ic50 76 2ug ml relative cisplatin however selectivity indic below all test cancer cell line indicat limit selectivity toward cancer cell relative normal human dermal fibroblast also show antibacterial activiti against five pathogenic strain four escherichia coli spp proteu mirabili conclusion vaginali contain constituent may responsible moderate anticancer antimicrobial activiti nevertheles low selectivity indic observ crude extract indicate limit cancer-cell specificity highlight need further fractionation isolation character active constituent before their therapeutic potential can fully assess vaginali phytochemical antimicrobial anticancer lc-esi-m ms antecedent ononi vaginali pertenece la familia fabaceae se distribuye en la region mediterranea objetivo este estudio se centro en la elucidacion fitoquimica en la evaluacion de las actividad anticancerigena antimicrobiana de vaginali metodo el perfil fitoquimico se caracterizo mediante cromatografi,1.0.0,0c490fc959114c7f
2145-2660,Vitae+33-2++364067+(1).pdf,Vitae+33-2++364067+(1)_0,en,Yina Pájaro Gonzalez,"Background: Mammea americana L. (“mamey”) is a tropical plant traditionally used in South America for its therapeutic, antimicrobial, and insect-repellent properties. Its ethnopharmacological relevance and reported in vitro antibacterial activity support its potential application in topical pharmaceutical formulations with anti-infective properties. However, additional studies are needed to characterize the physicochemical behavior of its extracts. Objective: This study aimed to evaluate the physicochemical and phytochemical properties of an ethanolic extract obtained from the endosperm of M. americana and assess its suitability for topical antimicrobial formulations. Methods: Two ethanolic extracts (EMa01 and EMa02) were prepared from dried and ground plant material with different particle size distributions determined by laser diffraction analysis to evaluate the effect of granulometry on the extraction process. The extracts were characterized by TLC, HPLCPDA, and UV-Vis spectroscopy. Solubility studies for EMa01 were conducted according to OECD guidelines using 18 formulation excipients, including solvents of different polarities, essential oils, surfactants, and pH conditions, in order to identify suitable components for topical formulations. In addition, Shinoda and ferric chloride assays and accelerated stability studies were performed. Results: EMa01 showed a higher extraction yield (7.75%) than EMa02 (4.83%), indicating that particle size influenced extraction efficiency, although TLC profiles revealed similar chemical compositions. HPLC analysis confirmed the presence of the coumarins mammea B/BA and mammea B/BC, compounds previously associated with antibacterial activity, which were selected as markers of extract quality and biological activity. Flavonoids and tannins were also detected in the extract. Among the evaluated excipients, Span 80 and rosemary oil exhibited the best solubility and compatibility with EMa01. Conclusions: These findings demonstrate that particle size affects extraction yield but not the chromatographic profile of the extract. Although the antibacterial activity of the extract was not evaluated in the present study, the detection of the coumarin mammea B/BA may serve as an indicator of potential pharmacological activity. The preformulation studies provide preliminary information supporting the future development of topical antimicrobial or insect-repellent formulations based on M. americana extract","Mammea americana; mammea coumarins; preformulation; solubility; topical delivery systems. ORIGINAL ARTICLE Published 21 July 2026 DOI: https://doi.org/10.17533/udea.vitae.v33n2a364067 2 Journal Vitae | https://revistas.udea.edu.co/index.php/vitae Volume 33 | Number 02 | Article 364067 Angie Pertuz Guerrero, Camilo Pérez Acuña, Reinaldo G. Sotomayor Pino, Fredyc Díaz Castillo and Yina Pájaro Gonz",marcadores,yina pajaro gonzalez,background mammea americana mamey tropical plant traditionally used south america its therapeutic antimicrobial insect-repellent properti its ethnopharmacological relevance report vitro antibacterial activity support its potential application topical pharmaceutical formul anti-infective properti however additional studi need characterize physicochemical behavior its extract objective study aimed evaluate physicochemical phytochemical properti ethanolic extract obtain endosperm americana asses its suitability topical antimicrobial formul method two ethanolic extract ema01 ema02 prepar dried ground plant material different particle size distribution determin laser diffraction analysi evaluate effect granulometry extraction proces extract characteriz tlc hplcpda uv-vi spectroscopy solubility studi ema01 conduct accord oecd guidelin using 18 formulation excipient includ solvent different polariti essential oils surfactant ph condition order identify suitable component topical formul addition 