2145-4604,CJA-2025-0101_ENG.pdf,CJA-2025-0101_ENG_0,en,e person performing the,"Introduction Despite being the second most frequent complication of neuraxial blocks, the incidence of post-dural puncture headache (PDPH) and its risk factors show wide variability in the scientific literature. Additionally, there are few studies addressing this issue in our setting Objective To determine the incidence, population characteristics, and risk factors associated with PDPH following spinal anesthesia in obstetric and non-obstetric patients at a university hospital in Bogotá, Colombia. Methods This is a prospective, descriptive, and analytical cohort study conducted in adult patients undergoing spinal anesthesia. The incidence of PDPH was calculated, and its association with risk factors was assessed using descriptive and bivariate analyses. Results A total of 186 patients were included, with a PDPH incidence of 10.8%. Regarding severity, 10% of the cases were mild, 50% moderate, and 40 % severe. A statistically significant association was found between PDPH and female sex (100% women; p = 0.0067), as well as with preeclampsia (OR 4.3; p = 0.01). No statistically significant differences were observed regarding other comorbidities, procedural characteristics, number of attempts, or operator expertise. Conclusions Although PDPH is usually self-limiting, the condition carries risks and is associated with reduced patient satisfaction. Institutional protocols should focus on at-risk populations and on implementing safe, evidence-based practices",Spinal anesthesia; Caesarean section; Post-dural puncture headache; Risk factors; Incidence. colombian journal of anesthesiology. 2026;54:e1190 2/10,marcadores,person perform,introduction despite being second most frequent complication neuraxial block incidence post-dural puncture headache pdph its risk factor show wide variability scientific literature additionally there few studi address issue our sett objective determine incidence population characteristic risk factor associat pdph follow spinal anesthesia obstetric non-obstetric patient university hospital bogota colombia method prospective descriptive analytical cohort study conduct adult patient undergo spinal anesthesia incidence pdph calculat its association risk factor assess using descriptive bivariate analys result total 186 patient includ pdph incidence 10 regard severity 10 case mild 50 moderate 40 severe statistically significant association found between pdph female sex 100 women 0067 well preeclampsia 01 no statistically significant differenc observ regard other comorbiditi procedural characteristic number attempt operator expertise conclusion although pdph usually self-limit condition carri risk associat reduc patient satisfaction institutional protocol should focu at-risk popul implement safe evidence-bas practic,spinal anesthesia caesarean section post-dural puncture headache risk factor incidence colombian journal anesthesiology 2026 54 e1190 10,person perform introduction despite being second most frequent complication neuraxial block incidence post-dural puncture headache pdph its risk factor show wide variability scientific literature additionally there few studi address issue our sett objective determine incidence population characteristic risk factor associat pdph follow spinal anesthesia obstetric non-obstetric patient university hospital bogota colombia method prospective descriptive analytical cohort study conduct adult patient undergo spinal anesthesia incidence pdph calculat its association risk factor assess using descriptive bivariate analys result total 186 patient includ pdph incidence 10 regard severity 10 case mild 50 moderate 40 severe statistically significant association found between pdph female sex 100 women 0067 well preeclampsia 01 no statistically significant differenc observ regard other comorbiditi procedural characteristic number attempt operator expertise conclusion although pdph usually self-limit cond