2145-7166,361211_ENG_V6.pdf,361211_ENG_V6_0,en,"1División Ficología “Dr. Sebastian A. Guarrera” Museo de La Plata, Facultad de Ciencias Naturales y Museo, Universidad","Wetlands are highly productive and complex, considered fragile ecosystems of great importance for conservation. The Ayapel swamp system is the second largest in Colombia. In the context of a study of the Ayapel Swamp diatom flora, we found that a large proportion of taxa could not be assigned to known species. Furthermore, identified species were mainly species endemic to the study area or to the Neotropics. Between 2006–2009, sampling was conducted spanning three hydrological cycles under different flood pulse conditions. Water column depth, transparency, temperature, dissolved oxygen, electrical conductivity, alkalinity, hardness, pH, and total depth were measured. Total dissolved solids, total solids, nitrates (N–NO3), nitrites (N–NO2), ammoniacal nitrogen (N–NH+ 4), reactive soluble phosphorus (P–PO-3 4), and sulfates (S–SO-2 4) were determined. Root samples were taken from the three macrophytes with the greatest cover, placed in independent plastic jars, filled with distilled water, and fixed with Lugol's solution. Samples were treated to remove organic matter. Treated materials were mounted for light microscopy (LM), and for scanning electron microscopy (SEM). The studied materials were deposited in the collection of the División Ficología del Museo de La Plata (LPC). Three new species are described as a result of this study: Eunotia ayapelensis nov. sp., Caloneis patiquensis nov. sp. and Gomphonema playaensis nov. sp","Bacillariophyceae, Caloneis patiquensis nov. sp., Eunotia ayapelensis nov. sp., Gomphonema playaensis nov. sp., wetlands",marcadores,1division ficologia dr sebastian guarrera museo de la plata facultad de ciencia natural museo universidad,wetland highly productive complex consider fragile ecosystem great importance conservation ayapel swamp system second largest colombia context