2145-8480,ION_Vol_38N3_25_ART6.pdf,ION_Vol_38N3_25_ART6_0,en,taron variaciones significativas,"The generation of biosolids in wastewater treatment plants represents an increasing environmental challenge in large urban centers such as Bogotá. In this city, the Salitre Wastewater Treatment Plant (WWTP) treats approximately one-third of the total wastewater flow, producing a significant amount of biosolids. These materials contain compounds potentially hazardous to public health, which limits their final disposal options. Among the emerging technologies for biosolid management and valorization, thermal treatments—particularly pyrolysis—have shown high potential. This process enables the transformation of waste into products with industrial applications, such as carbonaceous solids, organic liquids, and combustible gases. The present study aimed to evaluate the production of biochars through the pyrolysis of biosolids from the Salitre WWTP. An indirectly heated rotary kiln with a maximum processing capacity of 3.5 kg/h was used. Experiments were conducted at temperatures ranging from 250 to 700 °C to analyze the effect on product distribution and the energy content of each fraction. Mass and energy balances were performed to characterize the yields and properties of the solid, liquid, and gaseous fractions. The results showed that biochar was the fraction with the highest yield across the entire temperature range studied. Meanwhile, the liquid and gaseous fractions exhibited significant variations depending on temperature, suggesting opportunities to optimize the production of specific products according to the intended application. This study provides a technical basis for future techno-economic analyses and process scale-up studies, positioning pyrolysis as a viable alternative for the treatment and valorization of biosolids classified as hazardous waste",Biochar; Biosolids; Pirólisis; Rotary Kiln; Product Distribution. Pirólisis de biosólidos en un horno rotatorio a 