2145-8553,"Venas+hidrotermales+de+barita+en+rocas+metapelíticas+del+suroccidente+del+Macizo+de+Santander,+Colombia_+evidencias+de+ebullición+y+fluidos+moderadamente+salinos+ricos+en+sulfato.pdf","Venas+hidrotermales+de+barita+en+rocas+metapelíticas+del+suroccidente+del+Macizo+de+Santander,+Colombia_+evidencias+de+ebullición+y+fluidos+moderadamente+salinos+ricos+en+sulfato_0",en,Hydrothermal barite veins crosscutting metapelitic,"This study documents hydrothermal barite veins crosscutting pre-Devonian metapelitic rocks of the Chicamocha Schist Unit in the southwestern Santander Massif. Barite occurs as white-to-pink bladed crystals associated with minor calcite, Fe-oxides, and quartz, filling late-stage brittle fractures with a WNWESE strike. Qualitative XRD analyses confirm barite and calcite as the main mineral phases. Fluid inclusion petrography in barite reveals the coexistence, within the same fluid inclusion assemblages (FIAs), of liquidrich biphasic and monophase vapor inclusions with highly variable liquid-to-vapor ratios. These features indicate heterogeneous entrapment conditions, consistent with boiling processes during mineralization. Microthermometric data show first ice-melting temperatures near -40 °C, consistent with an H2O-NaClCaCl2 fluid system, and final ice-melting temperatures between -6.1 and -14.0 °C, corresponding to salinities of 9.3-17.8 mass % eq. NaCl. Homogenization temperatures display dispersion and high apparent values (>300 °C), reflecting the effects of heterogeneous entrapment. Raman spectroscopy of primary baritehosted fluid inclusions reveals a strong band at 980-983 cm⁻¹ attributed to sulfate ions in barite, together with additional peaks characteristic of internal vibrations of the SO4 2- in solution. Deconvolution of the O-H stretching band yields relative salinities of 13.6-21.6 mass% eq. NaCl, consistent with microthermometric estimates. Overall, the data indicate precipitation from moderately saline, sulfate-rich aqueous hydrothermal fluids. The lack of sulfur-bearing minerals in the host rocks suggests an evaporitic sulfur source, most likely from the Lower Cretaceous Rosablanca and Paja formations, transported by basinal fluids infiltrating the metamorphic basement. This contribution characterizes a previously unreported hydrothermal barite occurrence in the Santander Massif, providing new constraints on fluid circulation and late-stage hydrothermal processes in the Colombian Northern Andes","Chicamocha Schist; Hydrothermal fluids; Barite; Raman spectroscopy; Fluid Inclusion Microthermometry. Venas hidrotermales de barita en rocas metapelíticas del suroccidente del Macizo de Santander, Colombia: evidencias de ebullición y fluidos moderadamente salinos ricos en sulfato",marcadores,hydrothermal barite vein crosscutt metapelitic,study document hydrothermal barite vein crosscutt pre-devonian metapelitic rock chicamocha schist unit southwestern santander massif barite occur white-to-pink blad crystal associat minor calcite fe-oxid quartz fill late-stage brittle fractur wnwese strike qualitative xrd analys confirm barite calcite main mineral phas fluid inclusion petrography barite reveal coexistence within same fluid inclusion assemblag fias liquidrich biphasic monophase vapor inclusion highly variable liquid-to-vapor ratio these featur indicate heterogeneou entrapment condition consistent boil process during mineral microthermometric data show first ice-melt temperatur near -40 degc consistent h2o-naclcacl2 fluid system final ice-melt temperatur between -6 -14 degc correspond saliniti 3-17 mass eq nacl homogen temperatur display dispersion high apparent valu 300 degc reflect effect heterogeneou entrapment raman spectroscopy primary baritehost fluid inclusion reveal strong band 980-983 cm-1 attribut sulfate ions barite together additional peak characteristic internal vibr so4 2- solution deconvolution o-h stretch band yield relative saliniti 13 6-21 mass eq nacl consistent microthermometri