2215-7840,document (5).pdf,document (5)_0,en,"olombia. Revista Ciencias Biomédicas, 14(2),","Introduction: there are few studies on acid-base disorders in patients with sickle cell disease, and no studies were found in the pediatric population with this disease. Sickle cell disease is an inherited hemoglobin disorder characterized by the presence of hemoglobin S. Very few studies report acid-base disorders in these patients, especially in the pediatric population. It has been proposed that acidosis and hyperosmolarity induce structural changes in erythrocytes under hypoxic conditions, promoting vasoocclusive crisis. Vaso-occlusive crisis is the main manifestation and cause of hospitalization in these patients, with acidosis being one of its triggers. Objective: the objective of this study was to determine the acid-base status of pediatric patients diagnosed with sickle cell disease. Methods: a retrospective, cross-sectional study was conducted in which patients under 18 years of age with sickle cell disease who attended the Nephrology Department of the Napoleón Franco Pareja Hospital in Cartagena, Colombia, over a period of one year, with arterial blood gas reports, were included. Results: forty-three patients with a median age of 7 years (IQR 5–11) were included. Their medical records were analyzed; twenty-five patients (58.1%) presented metabolic acidosis, 22 of them (88%) had an elevated anion gap. No significant associations were found between acid-base status and sex or type of hemoglobinopathy (p>0.05). Conclusions: in the cases studied, it was found that most patients had metabolic acidosis and almost all of them had an elevated anion gap",Sickle cell disease; acid-base status; metabolic acidosis; anion gap; pediatrics,marcadores,olombia revista ciencia biomedica 14,introduction there few studi acid-base disorder patient sickle cell disease no studi found pediatric population disease sickle cell disease inherit hemoglobin disorder characteriz presence hemoglo