2216-1368,Art+3925+listo.pdf,Art+3925+listo_0,en,de la energía en cada volumen,"The geothermal gradient describes the variation of temperature with depth in the Earth's crust and is a key parameter for assessing geothermal energy potential. This study models terrestrial heat conduction by solving the thermal diffusion equation in spherical coordinates using the finite volume method (FVM). This approach ensures local conservation of energy within each control volume and provides a physically consistent approximation of the radial temperature profile. An explicit numerical scheme was implemented in MATLAB, discretizing the Earth's radius into eight nodes and simulating the temporal evolution of PROSPECTIVA VOL. 24 NÚMERO 2 temperature over one hour with a time step of 300 s. The results indicate a temperature decrease from 7000 K at the core to approximately 925 K at the surface after the simulated interval, yielding a geothermal gradient consistent with values reported in geophysical studies. The method proves stable, conservative, and suitable for numerical analysis of geothermal gradients, offering a useful tool for both scientific applications and educational purposes","geothermal gradient, heat conduction, partial differential equation, finite volume",marcadores,de la energia en cada volumen,geothermal gradient describ variation temperature depth earth crust key parameter assess geothermal energy potential study model terrestrial heat conduction solv thermal diffusion equation spherical coordinat using finite volume method fvm approach ensur local conservation energy within each control volume provid physically consistent approximation radial temperature profile explicit numerical scheme implement matlab discretiz earth radiu into eight node simulat temporal evolution prospectiva vol 24 numero temperature over one hour time step 300 result indicate temperature decrease 7000 core approximately 925 surface after simulat interval yield geothermal gradient consistent