2256-2958,359311_Ok.pdf,359311_Ok_0,en,Rev Colomb Cienc Pecu; 39(Jan-Dec):e359311,"Background: Skeletal development and the occurrence of skeletal deformities in fish represent critical issues that must be addressed to enhance the overall success and sustainability of aquaculture. Skeletal deformities negatively affect fish survival, growth performance, and nutritional efficiency. Objective: This study aimed to investigate skeletal deformities in juvenile Oncorhynchus mykiss, the most extensively farmed trout species in Turkey. Methods: A total of thirty trout farms were surveyed, and 1200 juvenile specimens were collected and examined. To identify skeletal deformities in the collected samples, the observed anomalies were classified into specific groups, and the most frequent pathological findings were evaluated. Results: The predominant skeletal deformity was compression (C), observed in 49.83% of the specimens. Regional analysis revealed that deformities were most prevalent in the caudal lordosis-kyphosis region (73.17%). Furthermore, lordosis was detected in 61% of the specimens examined. Among cranial and appendicular deformities, jawbone deformities showed the highest incidence (12.08%), followed by fin and pterygophore deformities. The most common pathological manifestation among the affected fish was impaired swimming performance (37.58%). Additionally, variations in total deformity rates were associated with different water temperatures across farms. Conclusion: The highest deformation rate was 19.58% at 13°C. The prevalence and distribution of skeletal deformities underscore the necessity for enhanced environmental regulation and management strategies in aquaculture systems to mitigate developmental abnormalities and improve production performance",aquaculture; environmental factors; fish growth performance; Oncorhynchus mykiss; prevalence; rainbow trout; skeletal deformities; skeletal development; sustainability; water temperature. Etiología y prevalencia 