2256-2958,359535.pdf,359535_0,en,eISSN: 2256-2958,"Background: Mast Cell Tumor (MCT) is one of the most common neoplasms in dogs, and histological grading is critical for its prognosis. However, there is limited local information on the influence of factors such as sex, age, breed, and anatomic location of the lesion on the malignant grade of this neoplasm. These factors are important for risk assessment and understanding the biological behavior of the tumor. Objective: To evaluate the association between risk factors (sex, age, breed, anatomical location) and histological grade of MCTs diagnosed at the Laboratorio de Patología Animal of the Universidad de Antioquia between 2019 and 2023. Methods: A retrospective study based on histopathology reports was conducted. A total of 3,468 canine biopsies were identified, of which 363 cases were diagnosed by cutaneous MCTs. Data on the year of the report and tumor classification were included. Statistical analysis included relative frequencies, and the risk of developing low-grade (LG) or high-grade (HG) MCT was estimated using univariable analysis with the Fisher exact test to determine odds ratios (ORs) with 95% confidence intervals (CI). Results: In all canine MCT reports, 74.7% were LG and 25.3% were HG. Males and females presented a similar frequency of tumors regardless of grade (rounded up to 50%). Patients aged 5-8 years had a higher risk for LG-MCT (OR = 2.16, CI 1.32, 3.52), while those aged 9-12 years had a higher risk for HG-MCT (OR = 2.10, CI 1.30, 3.39). The French Bulldog breed had an increased risk for HG-MCT (OR = 5.77, CI 1.96, 16.96). Pitbull, Mixed, and Pug breeds were most frequently diagnosed with the neoplasm, with no risk for a specific histologic grade. The most common location was the trunk, with 23.9% of HG and 21.4% of LG cases, with no statistical differences. Conclusions: The findings allowed identification of the risk associated with age and breed for the development of more aggressive forms of the tumor; other variables, such as sex, did not have an influence. This information may be relevant for clinical management and early risk prediction",age; breed; canine; histologic grade; Kiupel’s classification; mast cell tumor; prognosis; risk factors; sex; skin. Factores de riesgo asociados al grado histológico del mastocitoma canino basado en la clasificación de malignidad de dos niveles de Kiupel Fatores de risco associados ao grau histológico do tumor de mastócitos canino com base na classificação de malignidade de dois níveis de Kiup,marcadores,eissn 2256-2958,background mast cell tumor mct one most common neoplasm dogs histological grad critical its prognosi however there limit local information influence factor such sex age breed anatomic location lesion malignant grade neoplasm these factor important risk assessment understand biological behavior tumor objective evaluate association between risk factor sex age breed anatomical location histological grade mcts diagnos laboratorio de patologia animal universidad de antioquia between 2019 2023 method retrospective study based histopathology report conduct total 468 canine biopsi identifi which 363 case diagnos cutaneou mcts data year report tumor classification includ statistical analysi includ relative frequenci risk develop low-grade lg high-grade hg mct estimat using univariable analysi fisher exact test determine odds ratio ors 95 confidence interval ci result all canine mct report 74 lg 25 hg male femal present similar frequency tumor regardles grade round up 50 patient aged 5-8 year had higher risk lg-mct 16 ci 32 52 while those aged 9-12 year had higher risk hg-mct 10 ci 30 39 french bulldog breed had increas risk hg-mct 77 ci 96 16 96 pitbull mixed pug breed most frequently diagnos neoplasm no risk specific histologic grade most common location trunk 23 hg 21 lg case no statistical differenc conclusion finding allow identification risk associat age breed development more aggressive form tumor other variabl such sex did not have influence information may relevant clinical management early risk prediction,age breed canine histologic grade kiupel classification mast cell tumor prognosi risk factor sex skin factor de riesgo asociado al grado histologico del mastocitoma canino basado en la clasificacion de malignidad de dos nivel de kiupel fator de risco associado ao grau histologico do tumor de mastocito canino com base na classificacao de malignidade de dois nivei de kiup,eissn 2256-2958 background mast cell tumor mct one most common neoplasm dogs histological grad critical its prognosi however there limit local information influence factor such sex age breed anatomic location lesion malignant grade neoplasm these factor important risk assessment understand biological behavior tumor objective evaluate association between risk factor sex age breed anatomical location histological grade mcts diagnos laboratorio de patologia animal universidad de antioquia between 2019 2023 method retrospective study based histopathology report conduct total 468 canine biopsi identifi which 363 case diagnos cutaneou mcts data year report tumor classification includ statistical analysi includ relative frequenci risk develop low-grade lg high-grade hg mct estimat using univariable analysi fisher exact test determine odds ratio ors 95 confidence interval ci result all canine mct report 74 lg 25 hg male femal present similar frequency tumor regardles grade round up 50 patient aged 5-8 year had higher risk lg-mct 16 ci 32 52 while those aged 9-12 year had higher risk hg-mct 10 ci 30 39 french bulldog breed had increas risk hg-mct 77 ci 96 16 96 pitbull mixed pug breed most frequently diagnos neoplasm no risk specific histologic grade most common location trunk 23 hg 21 lg case no statistical differenc conclusion finding allow identification risk associat age breed development more aggressive form tumor other variabl such sex did not have influence information may relevant clinical mana