2256-2958,361091.pdf,361091_0,en,eISSN: 2256-2958,"Background: The genus Chlamydia comprises obligate intracellular Gram-negative bacteria responsible for various diseases in animals and humans. In pigs, the species C. suis is transmitted via aerogenic, genital, and oral routes, with its pathogenicity associated with the conjunctival tissue, respiratory system, gastrointestinal tract, and reproductive system. In addition to C. suis, other Chlamydia species have been identified in pigs. In Mexico, chlamydiosis was considered an exotic disease until 2016, the year it was recognized as endemic in birds and ruminants; however, there are no reports of this disease in pigs. Objective: This study aimed to detect the presence of Chlamydia suis and Chlamydia abortus in sows that had experienced abortion and conjunctivitis. Methods: This study examined the presence of Chlamydia suis and Chlamydia abortus in pigs from four farms in central Mexico between 2016 and 2022, focusing on animals exhibiting reproductive issues, conjunctivitis, or diarrhea. A total of 141 samples, including vaginal exudates (122), conjunctival exudates (17), and feces (2), were analyzed using real-time PCR (qPCR) to detect Chlamydiaceae and specific Chlamydia species. Results: As a result, 57 samples were positive for the family Chlamydiaceae (40.42%). Within this group, 26 animals (45.61%) were identified with a specific Chlamydia species, while in 31 animals (54.39%) the infecting species could not be determined, highlighting the need for further genomic analysis. Among the identified species, 12 animals were positive exclusively for C. abortus, one animal was positive exclusively for C. suis, and 13 animals presented coinfection with C. abortus and C. suis. Conclusions: These results align with global reports on Chlamydia prevalence in pigs and underscore the significance of co-infections, which were observed in vaginal exudates, feces, and conjunctival samples. This study constituted the first report of porcine chlamydiosis in Mexico, raising concerns about potential health risks for pig farms and zoonotic implications for humans. The study emphasized the need for improved surveillance of Chlamydia species across production stages and advocates further research into the genetic diversity and prevalence of Chlamydia in Mexico","Chlamydia abortus; Chlamydia suis; coinfection; pigs. Chlamydia suis y Chlamydia abortus en cerdos de México: primer reporte Chlamydia suis e Chlamydia abortus em suínos do México: primeiro relato Paola-Alexis Hernández-Ramírez1 ; María-Magdalena Limón-González1 ; Nelson Pérez-Romero2 ; Gabriela Palomares-Resendiz3 ; Rigoberto Hernández-Castro4 ; Efrén Díaz-Aparicio3 , Beatriz Arellano-Reynoso1*",marcadores,eissn 2256-2958,background genu chlamydia compris obligate intracellular gram-negative bacteria responsible variou diseas animal human pigs speci suis transmitt via aerogenic genital oral rout its pathogenicity associat conjunctival tissue respiratory system gastrointestinal tract reproductive system addition suis other chlamydia speci have been identifi pigs mexico chlamydiosi consider exotic disease until 2016 year recogniz endemic bird ruminant however there no report disease pigs objective study aimed detect presence chlamydia suis chlamydia abortu sows had experienc abortion conjunctiviti method study examin presence chlamydia suis chlamydia abortu pigs four farm central mexico between 2016 2022 focus animal exhibit reproductive issu conjunctiviti diarrhea total 141 sampl includ vaginal exudat 122 conjunctival exudat 17 fece analyz using real-time pcr qpcr detect chlamydiaceae specific chlamydia speci result result 57 sampl positive family chlamydiaceae 40 42 within group 26 animal 45 61 identifi specific chlamydia speci while 31 animal 54 39 infect speci could not determin highlight need further genomic analysi among identifi speci 12 animal positive exclusively abortu one animal positive exclusively suis 13 animal present coinfection abortu suis conclusion these result align global report chlamydia prevalence pigs underscore significance co-infection which observ vaginal exudat fece conjunctival sampl study constitut first report porcine chlamydiosi mexico rais concern about potential health risk pig farm zoonotic implic human study emphasiz need improv surveillance chlamydia speci acros production stag advocat further research into genetic diversity prevalence chlamydia mexico,chlamydia abortu chlamydia suis coinfection pigs chlamydia suis chlamydia abortu en cerdo de mexico primer reporte chlamydia suis chlamydia abortu em suino do mexico primeiro relato paola-alexi hernandez-ramirez1 maria-magdalena limon-gonzalez1 nelson perez-romero2 gabriela palomares-resendiz3 rigoberto hernandez-castro4 efren diaz-aparicio3 beatriz arellano-reynoso1,eissn 2256-2958 background genu chlamydia compris obligate intracellular gram-negative bacteria responsible variou diseas animal human pigs speci suis transmitt via aerogenic genital oral rout its pathogenicity associat conjunctival tissue respiratory system gastrointestinal tract reproductive system addition suis other chlamydia speci have been identifi pigs mexico chlamydiosi consider exotic disease until 2016 year recogniz endemic bird ruminant however there no report disease pigs objective study aimed detect presence chlamydia suis chlamydia abortu sows had experienc abortion conjunctiviti method study examin presence chlamydia suis chlamydia abortu pigs four farm central mexico between 2016 2022 focus animal exhibit reproductive issu conjunctiviti diarrhea total 141 sampl includ vaginal exudat 122 conjunctival exudat 17 fece analyz using real-time pcr qpcr detect chlamydiaceae specific chlamydia speci result result 57 sampl positive family chlamydiaceae 40 42 within group 26 animal 45 61 identifi specific chlamydia speci while 31 animal 54 39 infect speci could not determin highlight need further genomic analysi among identifi speci 12 animal positive exclusively abortu one animal positive exclusively suis 13 animal present coinfection abortu suis conclusion these result align global report chlamydia prevalence pigs underscore significance co-infection which observ vaginal exudat fece conjunctival sampl study constitut first report porcine chlamydiosi mexico rais concern about potential health risk pig farm zoonotic implic human study emphasiz need improv surveillance chlamydia speci acros production stag advocat further research into genetic diversity prevalence chlamydia mexico chlamydia abortu chlamydia suis coinfection pigs chlamydia suis chlamydia abortu en cerdo de mexico primer reporte chlamydia suis chlamydia abortu em suino do mexico primeiro relato paola-alexi hernandez-ramirez1 maria-magdalena limon-gonzalez1 nelson perez-romero2 gabriela palomares-resendiz3 rigoberto hernandez-castro4 efren diaz-aparicio3 beatriz arellano-reynoso1,1.0.0,7d2c9de9da47f200
2256-2958,361091.pdf,361091_0,es,"icio E, Arellano-Reynoso B.","Antecedentes: Chlamydia es un género de bacterias gramnegativas, intracelulares obligadas, que causan diversas enfermedades en animales y humanos. En cerdos, C. suis se transmite por vías aerógena, genital y oral, afectando el tejido conjuntival, el sistema respiratorio, el tracto gastrointestinal y el sistema reproductivo. Además de C. suis, se han identificado otras especies de Chlamydia en cerdos. En México, la clamidiosis fue considerada exótica hasta 2016, cuando se reconoció como endémica en aves y rumiantes; sin embargo, no hay reportes en cerdos. Objetivo: Detectar la presencia de Chlamydia suis y Chlamydia abortus en cerdas con antecedentes de aborto y conjuntivitis. Métodos: Se investigó la presencia de C. suis y C. abortus en cerdos de cuatro granjas del centro de México entre 2016 y 2022, priorizando animales con problemas reproductivos, conjuntivitis o diarrea. Se analizaron 141 muestras: exudados vaginales (122), conjuntivales (17) y heces (2), mediante PCR en tiempo real (qPCR) para detectar Chlamydiaceae y especies específicas de Chlamydia. Resultados: Los resultados mostraron que 57 muestras fueron positivas para la familia Chlamydiaceae (40.42%). Dentro de este grupo, 26 animales (45.61%) fueron identificados con una especie específica de Chlamydia, mientras que en 31 animales (54.39%) no fue posible determinar la especie infectante, lo que resalta la necesidad de análisis genómicos adicionales. Entre las especies identificadas, 12 animales fueron positivos exclusivamente a C. abortus, un animal fue positivo exclusivamente a C. suis y 13 animales presentaron coinfección con C. abortus y C. suis. Conclusiones: Estos hallazgos coinciden con informes globales sobre la prevalencia de Chlamydia en cerdos y resaltan la importancia de las coinfecciones. Este estudio constituye el primer informe sobre clamidiosis porcina en México, lo que plantea preocupaciones sobre su impacto en las granjas y su potencial zoonótico. Se destaca la necesidad de mejorar la vigilancia de Chlamydia en la producción porcina y de ampliar la investigación sobre su diversidad genética y prevalencia en México",cerdos; Chlamydia abortus; Chlamydia suis; coinfección,marcadores,icio arellano-reynoso,antecedent chlamydia genero bacteri gramnegativ intracelular obligad causan divers enfermedad animal human cerd suis transmite vias aerogena genital oral afect tejido conjuntival sistema respiratorio tracto gastrointestinal sistema reproductivo adem suis han identificado otras especi chlamydia cerd mexico clamidiosis fue considerada exotica hasta 2016 cuando reconocio como endemica aves rumiant sin embargo no hay report cerd objetivo detectar presencia chlamydia suis chlamydia abortus cerd antecedent aborto conjuntivitis metod investigo presencia suis abortus cerd cuatro granj centro mexico entre 2016 2022 prioriz animal problem reproductiv conjuntivitis diarrea analizaron 141 muestr exudad vaginal 122 conjuntival 17 heces mediante pcr tiempo real qpcr detectar chlamydiaceae especi especific chlamydia resultad resultad mostraron 57 muestr fueron positiv familia chlamydiaceae 40 42 dentro este grupo 26 animal 45 61 fueron identificad especie especifica chlamydia mientr 31 animal 54 39 no fue posible determinar especie infectante resalta necesidad analisis genomic adicional entre especi identificad 12 animal fueron positiv exclusiva abortus animal fue positivo exclusiva suis 13 animal presentaron coinfeccion abortus suis conclusion estos hallazg coinciden inform global sobre prevalencia chlamydia cerd resaltan importancia coinfeccion este estudio constituye primer informe sobre clamidiosis porcina mexico plantea preocup sobre impacto granj potencial zoonotico destaca necesidad mejorar vigilancia chlamydia produccion porcina ampliar investigacion sobre diversidad genetica prevalencia mexico,cerd chlamydia abortus chlamydia suis coinfeccion,icio arellano-reynoso antecedent chlamydia genero 