2256-3334,360926_final.pdf,360926_final_0,en,talización/unidad de cuidados,"Objective: To determine the relationship between sociodemographic variables and the diagnosis of dengue with warning signs in children and adolescents aged 0 to 17 years in the department of Quindío during 2024. Methods: An observational, analytical cross-sectional study was conducted. The study sample consisted of 3,065 cases of dengue in children under 17 years of age from the department of Quindío, reported to the National Public Health Surveillance System during 2024. The association between the dependent variable (classification of dengue with or without warning signs) and sociodemographic variables was analyzed. A binary logistic regression model was constructed to estimate adjusted odds ratios (OR), with their respective 95% confidence intervals (CI). Results: The main finding reveals that adolescents aged 12–17 years had a significantly higher probability of developing dengue with warning signs compared to children in early childhood (0–5 years) (adjusted OR = 1.383; 95% CI: 1.010–1.894). Variables such as final clinical management (hospitalization/intensive care unit) and a shorter notification time (0–3 days) also showed a strong association with the outcome. Conclusion: Belonging to the adolescent age group is an independent and statistically significant risk factor for developing dengue with warning signs in the pediatric population. This association, which persists after adjusting for other sociodemographic factors, suggests an intrinsic vulnerability in this age group, which is crucial to consider for epidemiological surveillance and clinical management","dengue, adolescent health, children's health, sociodemographic variables. Relação entre variáveis ​sociodemográficas e dengue com sinais de alerta em crianças e adolescentes",marcadores,talizacion unidad de cuidado,objective determine relationship between sociodemographic variabl diagnosi dengue warn sign children adolescent a