2256-3334,363361_final.pdf,363361_final_0,en,Dussan-Chaux JD. Agroecología: estrategia,"In Colombia, conventional agriculture based on the intensive use of agrochemicals constitutes a priority public health problem, with disproportionate impacts on rural and indigenous communities due to occupational, domestic, and environmental exposure. Globally, an estimated 2 to 5 million acute pesticide poisonings occur annually, and chronic exposure is associated with cancer and neurological and reproductive disorders. In 2022, Colombia registered an incidence of 12.7 cases per 100,000 inhabitants, with rural morbidity three times higher than urban morbidity, in addition to a significant number of poisonings with suicidal intent and mortality attributed to highly hazardous herbicides. Exposure is exacerbated by unsafe practices, indoor storage, inadequate personal protective equipment, and aerial spraying with glyphosate in indigenous territories, with reports of dermatological, respiratory, and reproductive health problems. This essay argues that agroecology, as a science, practice, and social movement, is a strategy that promotes health by strengthening food and nutritional sovereignty, social cohesion and territorial governance, ecosystem resilience, and reducing pesticide exposure. It also contributes to the prevention of self-inflicted violence by decreasing access to lethal substances. It concludes that promoting agroecological transitions with institutional support and regulation of highly hazardous pesticides is a relevant intersectoral strategy for reducing inequalities and improving well-being in rural areas","agroecology, pesticides, health promotion, indigenous population health, public health. Agroecologia: estratégia para promoção da saúde em comunidades camponesas e indígenas da Colômbia",marcadores,dussan-chaux jd agroecologia estrategia,colombia conventional agriculture based intensive use agrochemical constitut priority public health problem disproportionate 