2256-3334,363375_final.pdf,363375_final_0,en,Milk safety in the household: Antibiotic residue,"Objective: To determine the presence of antibiotic residues in bovine milk destined for household consumption in smallholder dairy units in Abejorral (Antioquia, Colombia), in 2023, and to characterize associated herd management and domestic milk handling practices. Methods: A cross-sectional exploratory study was conducted on 20 smallholder dairy farms selected through non-probabilistic snowball sampling. Forty milk samples (20 pre-hygiene and 20 post-hygiene samples) were collected and screened for sulfonamides, tetracyclines, beta-lactams, and ceftiofur. Information on herd management and household milk handling practices was obtained through a structured questionnaire administered to producers. Results: No sample contained more than one antibiotic simultaneously in either phase. Overall, 5/20 (25%) pre-hygiene and 4/20 (20%) post-hygiene samples were positive for at least one antibiotic residue. Tetracycline was detected in 4/20 (20%) pre-hygiene samples and no post-hygiene, whereas sulfonamide was detected in 1/20 (5%) prehygiene and 4/20 (20%) post-hygiene samples. Beta-lactams were detected in 1/20 (5%) samples in both phases, and ceftiofur was not detected. Residues detected before hygienization disappeared afterward in two units (10%), whereas residues appeared only after hygienization in one unit. Among the farm practices, 55% of the units reported mastitis during the previous week, none held good farming practices certification, and 60% reported boiling milk before consumption. No significant differences were observed. Conclusion: In this sample of smallholder farms, a notable fraction of household milk contained antibiotic residues, with class-specific changes after domestic hygienization. These findings support the strengthening of antimicrobial stewardship, withdrawal period compliance, and milk safety interventions at the household level","Antibiotic residues, household milk consumption, hygiene practices, food safety, public health. Volumen 44, 2026 DOI: https://doi.org/10.17533/udea.rfnsp. e363375 Recibido: 26/12/2025 Aprobado: 22/05/2025 Publicado: 26/06/2026 Cita: Aristizábal-Parra E, Portilla-Lizcano P, DávilaDávila J, Correa-Valencia NM. Milk safety in the household: Antibiotic residue detection and management practices in",marcadores,milk safety household antibiotic residue,objective determine presence antibiotic residu bovine milk destin household consumption smallholder dairy unit abejorral antioquia colombia 2023 characterize associat herd management domestic milk handl practic method cross-sectional exploratory study conduct 20 smallholder dairy farm select through non-probabilistic snowball sampl forty milk sampl 20 pre-hygiene 20 post-hygiene sampl collect screen sulfonamid tetracyclin beta-lactam ceftiofur information herd management household milk handl practic obtain through structur questionnaire administer producer result no sample contain more than one antibiotic simultaneously either phase overall 20 25 pre-hygiene 20 20 post-hygiene sampl positive least one antibiotic residue tetracycline detect 20 20 pre-hygiene sampl no post-hygiene wherea sulfonamide detect 20 prehygiene 20 20 post-hygiene sampl beta-lactam detect 20 sampl both phas ceftiofur not detect residu detect before hygien disappear afterward two unit 10 wherea residu appear only after hygien one unit among farm practic 55 unit report mastiti during previou week none held good farm practic certification 60 report boil milk before consumption no significant differenc observ conclusion sample smallholder farm notable fraction household milk contain antibiotic residu class-specific chang after domestic hygien these finding support strengthen antimicrobial stewardship withdrawal period compliance milk safety intervention household level,antibiotic residu household milk consumption hygiene practic food safety public health volumen 44 2026 doi