2256-5248,Rhela-28-48-m-207-268-21-62.pdf,Rhela-28-48-m-207-268-21-62_0,en,"revela transiciones del oralismo excluyente al bilingüismo, con persistencia de","Objective: The aim is to critically analyze how the training, regulation and participation of Colombian Sign Language (LSC) interpreters have influenced the guarantee of inclusive education for deaf people in Colombia, and to identify patterns in the implementation of public policies from an intercultural and linguistic rights perspective. Originality/Contribution: We propose a new historical periodization of the educational-communicative evolution of the Colombian deaf community, dividing it into five stages: prehistory of sign language; imposition of oralism 1880-1950; associative movement 1950-1985; auditory rehabilitation vs. recognition of Colombian Sign Language 1960-2000; and training of interpreters post-2000. This expands previous frameworks and makes visible tensions between policies, educational practices and professional training. Method: This cross-sectional documentary review examines the evolution of historical and normative milestones in the recognition of LSC (by its Spanish acronym), as well as the role of interpreters. It also considers the tensions between public policies, educational practice, and training in school contexts, and the challenges involved in applying and implementing interpretation services in view of the communicative diversity of the deaf population. Strategies/Data Collection: A systematic review of primary and secondary sources was conducted, including Colombian regulations (laws, decrees, and resolutions), institutional documents from INSOR and FENASCOL, 19th-century historiographical sources (Congress of Milan, 1880), medical-pedagogical archives, oral testimonies from leaders of the deaf community, historical census records, and specialized academic literature on deaf education, Colombian Sign Language (LSC) linguistics, and interpreter training. The search encompassed institutional repositories, academic databases, and historical archives, with a timeframe extending from pre-Columbian times to 2025. Conclusions: In order to move towards bilingual intercultural education that guarantees communicative equity from early childhood, linguistic policies are needed to strengthen the ongoing training of interpreters, early immersion in LSC and the leading role of the deaf community","colombian sign language; history of education; sign language interpreters; bilingual education; oralism; language policies. 230 Rev. hist.edu.latinoam - Vol. 28 No. 48, enero - junio 2026 - ISSN: 0122-7238 - pp. 227 - 268 Gina Viviana Morales-Acosta, José Antonio Marcelino, Fredy Daniel Luque Herreño, Jorge Andrés Tobón Villarreal, Lina Fernanda Mejía Rojas Proposta de trajetórias educativo-com",marcadores,revela transicion del oralismo excluyente al bilinguismo con persistencia de,objective aim critically analyze how train regulation participation colombian sign language lsc interpreter have influenc guarantee inclusive education deaf people colombia identify pattern implementation public polici intercultural linguistic right perspective originality contribution we propose new historical period educational-communicative evolution colombian deaf community divid into five stag prehistory sign language imposition oralism 1880-1950 associative movement 1950-1985 auditory rehabilitation vs recognition colombian sign language 1960-2000 train interpreter post-2000 expand previou framework make visible tension between polici educational practic professional train method cross-sectional documentary review examin evolution historical normative mileston recognition lsc its spanish acronym well role interpreter also consider tension between public polici educational practice train school context challeng involv apply implement interpretation servic view communicative diversity deaf population strategi data collection systematic review primary secondary sourc conduct i