2256-5442,TAC_+RCGeo_35-2+_AGO06+V3.pdf,TAC_+RCGeo_35-2+_AGO06+V3_14,en,"México, México.","Due to gender mandates, caregiving has been assigned to women without social recognition, and most of the time the work is unpaid because it is rooted in relationships of “love”, which from the outset creates an injustice. School education, including traditional geography, has contributed to reproducing these roles from a Eurocentric, binary, and extractivist perspective that subordinates both women and nature. However, the classroom can also be a space of resistance. This paper is based on a documentary analysis guided by the question: What can be done by high school geography to promote a new social organization of care? The proposal identifies four lines of action: questioning gender roles to foster shared responsibility for care (among individuals, communities, companies, and the state); building community through territory; promoting self-care; and valuing life over capital accumulation. We consider it urgent to incorporate these perspectives into geographical education, making care visible as a central axis in the struggle for social, environmental, and economic justice-rather than relegating it to the private sphere","care, teaching, education, feminisms, geography. Highlights: reflective article that systematizes proposals from various authors to promote a care-centered education through four areas of classroom intervention: questioning gender roles, building community through territory, fostering self-care, and prioritizing life over the logic of capitalist accumulation. Por uma nova organização social do",marcadores,mexico mexico,due gender mandat caregiv has been assign women without social recognition most time work unpaid because root relationship love which outset creat injustice school education includ traditional geography has contribut reproduc these role eurocentric binary extractivist perspective subordinat both women nature however classroom can also space 