2357-4720,HyS51.pdf,HyS51_5,en,los aportes y comentarios,"| This article explores the process of legal exclusion of women from culinary and hospitality labor in Buenos Aires, Argentina, during the first half of the twentieth century. It argues that the male appropriation of occupations such as cooks, maîtres d’hotel, and waiters required both the exclusion of women and the construction of these tasks as inappropriate for them. Various arguments contributed to shaping the culinary sector as a morally unsuitable environment for women, citing its development in the public sphere, long working hours, risks of accidents, night work, proximity to alcoholic beverages, and contact with a predominantly male clientele. The analysis relies on a corpus of sources including regulations, parliamentary debates, petitions to the Chamber of Deputies, the Information Bulletin of the National Department of Labor, and academic theses on women’s and children’s labor. These are supplemented by journalistic articles, advertisements, classified ads, and labor court rulings. The findings highlight how gender inequalities and hierarchies were historically constructed in the labor market, as well as the crucial role of spatial and temporal dimensions: while in certain spaces and times women could—and were expected to— perform tasks like cooking and serving, in others, they were virtually barred from doing so","gastronomy; nightlife; alcohol; gender; Argentina; 20th century. Proteção do corpo e da moral: exclusão das mulheres do trabalho no setor gastronômico (Buenos Aires, Argentina, primeira metade do século XX)*",marcadores,los aport comentario,article explor proces legal exclusion women culinary hospitality labor bueno aire argentina during first half twentieth century argu male appropriation occup such cook maitr hotel waiter requir both exclusion women construction these task inappropriate them variou argument contribut shap culinary sector morally unsuitable environment women citing its