2357-4720,HyS51.pdf,HyS51_7,en,formación); investigación (tratamiento,"| This article examines the development of the educational radio in Colombia (1930–1946) as a contested dimension of the cultural modernization project pursued during the Liberal Republic. Its objective is to analyze the regulation and utilization of this broadcasting medium by the state, commercial broadcasters and the Catholic Church. Using a social and institutional history framework, the article draws on a triangulation of primary sources including ministerial reports, journals, and newspapers to contrast normative discourse with material realities. The findings reveal a structural gap between pedagogical ambition and operational capacity. Unlike the coordinated deployment of cinema, radio remained fragmented given the absence of rural electrical infrastructure and the urban concentration of radio receivers. The article demonstrates that the consolidation of the National Broadcasting Service (Radiodifusora Nacional) in 1940 constituted a belated attempt to contest cultural hegemony in the face of commercial broadcasting. It concludes that liberal radio functioned as an incomplete technology of governance, remaining fundamentally urban despite its aspiration to become a rural medium. This experience revealed the inability of the secular state to monopolize distance education, facilitating the Catholic Church’s consolidation of its educational hegemony from 1947 onward",| educational radio; Liberal Republic period; rural education; modernization; Colombia; twentieth century. La rádio urbana que sonhava em ser rural. Paradoxos da educação radiofônica na República Liberal (1930-1946),marcadores,formacion investigacion tratamiento,article examin development educational radio colombia 1930-1946 contest dimension cultural modern project pursu during liberal republic its objective analyze regulation util broadcast medium state commercial broadcaster catholic church using social institutional history 