2357-626X,3-6931-Residenando+aldeas_2026.pdf,3-6931-Residenando+aldeas_2026_0,es,"ta (Google Maps, planos, fotografías) y referentes internacionales","Cómo citar | 55 REVISTA DE (BOGOTÁ) 56 | Abstract * Arquitecta, Universidad Nacional de Ingeniería. Lima (Perú). Filiación institucional: Universidad Nacional de Ingeniería, Facultad de Arquitectura, Urbanismo y Artes. Lima (Perú). https://scholar.google.com/citations?hl=es&authuser=1&user=ysHX0usAAAAJ https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2748-8627 fmechatol@uni.pe / fiorella0301@gmail.com Thousands of children and adolescents in situations of abandonment live in infrastructures that, while functional, do not promote their overall well-being or emotional development. In this context, the present article reflects on the architecture of children’s villages and similar typologies in Peru, proposing a typological reconfiguration with an architectural-landscape approach grounded in pedagogical principles. The aim is to identify design criteria that transform these spaces into educational, stimulating, and well-being-oriented environments. The literature review encompassed approaches such as neuroarchitecture, Montessori, Waldorf, and Patio Vivo pedagogies, as well as national and international regulatory guidelines related to the typology under study. The research adopted a qualitative exploratory approach, based on the analysis of multiple cases, combining indirect observation (Google Maps, plans, photographs) with international references in Europe, Asia, and Latin America. The study comprised three phases: diagnosis of Peruvian children’s villages, review of global references, and formulation of spatial guidelines. Among the findings, an underuse of outdoor areas, limited diversity of uses, and poor landscape integration were identified. In contrast, international cases reveal spatial strategies that promote free play, autonomy, and connection with nature. Finally, five design dimensions are proposed to guide the redesign of these spaces as inclusive, active, and enriching environments for vulnerable children and their personal development","childcare services; child development; landscape architecture; learning environments; outdoor spaces · Fiorella Margarita Mechato-Lara (Bogotá) VOL 28 · Nº 2 · JULIO-DICIEMBRE 2026 · ISSN: 1657-0308 · E-ISSN: 2357-626X CAR (Centro de Acogida Residencial): “Espacio físico administrado por una institución pública, privada, mixta o comunal donde viven niñas, niños y adolescentes en situación de aba",marcadores,ta google maps plan fotografi referent internacional,como citar 55 revista bogota 56 abstract arquitecta universidad nacional ingenieria lima peru filiacion institucional universidad nacional ingenieria facultad arquitectura urbanismo artes lima peru https scholar google com citations hl authuser user yshx0usaaaaj https orcid org 0000-0003-2748-8627 fmechatol uni pe fiorella0301 gmail com thousands of children and adolescents in situations of abandonment live in infrastructur that while functional do not promote their overall well-being or emotional development in this context the present article reflects on the architecture of children villag and similar typologi in peru proposing typological reconfiguration with an architectural-landscape approach grounded in pedagogical principl the aim is to identify design criteria that transform these spac into educational stimulating and well-being-oriented environments the literature review encompassed approach such as neuroarchitecture montessori waldorf and patio vivo pedagogi as well as national and international regulatory guidelin related to the typology under study the research adopted qualitative exploratory approach based on the analysis of multiple cases combining indirect observation google maps plans photographs with international referenc in europe asia and latin america the study comprised three phas diagnosis of peruvian children villag review of global referenc and formulation of spatial guide