2382-4581,articulo+3+ID_733+correccion+220826.pdf,articulo+3+ID_733+correccion+220826_0,en,REUSE POTENTIAL.,"Efficient and sustainable technologies for disinfection of domestic wastewater are critical for safeguarding public health and the environment, as pathogens and contaminants threaten human well-being and ecological balance. The application of UV radiation is considered an environmentally sound alternative, as it does not alter the physicochemical characteristics of treated effluents nor produce hazardous by-products. Nevertheless, concerns persist about the durability of UV lamps under adverse conditions, the susceptibility of systems to mechanical failure, and reduced efficiency derived from suspended solids that induce clogging, fouling and attenuation of UV radiation. The study assesses the impact of suspended solids by comparing unfiltered wastewater (UFWW) and filtered wastewater (FWW) under low (471 mW∙s/ cm²), medium (941 mW·s/cm²), and high (3722 mW·s/cm²) UV doses at 254 nm. Disinfection efficiencies were nearly 99% across all treatments, indicating that solids concentration (<50mg/L) does not significantly impair UV performance. E. coli inactivation, measured by most probable number (MPN), achieved efficiencies of ≥99.9% in all cases. Logarithmic inactivation values ranged from 2 to 4 for TABC, MPN, and qPCR, confirming that higher UV doses enhance microbial inactivation. This study also evaluates the effectiveness of microbiological (Total Aerobic Bacteria Count – TABC) and molecular (quantitative real-time polymerase chain reaction – qPCR). Results showed that qPCR, targeting the rpoB gene, detected higher microbial recovery (2 log) compared to TABC, highlighting its superior sensitivity. The study underscores the advantages of UV disinfection, including its efficiency, compact design, and cost-effectiveness compared to chemical methods",/ PALABRAS CLAVE AFFILIATION EVALUACIÓN DE UN SISTEMA UV PARA LA DESINFECCIÓN DE AGUAS RESIDUALES DOMÉSTICAS CO