2389-8526,5345_VF.pdf,5345_VF_0,en,lente en la población afectada (n = 37). Las lesiones,"This study was conducted with the aim of collecting prognostic factors and evaluating clinical and paraclinical parameters in felines diagnosed with squamous cell carcinoma (scc) over a period of two years (2020-2022). scc is the most common malignant skin tumor in cats and is considered to have a multifactorial origin, with ultraviolet (uv) ra diation exposure, breed, and the cat’s habitat being the main risk factors. The analysis in cluded variables related to laboratory tests (complete blood count and blood chemistry), as well as the location and type of lesions, assessing their impact on survival. Regarding 2 Nicolás Camargo Puentes • César Augusto Díaz • Laura Montejo Botero • Frank Harry Suárez Sánchez Rev. Med. Vet. issn 0122-9354. issne 2389-8526: Bogotá (Colombia) n.° 52: e5345 • enero-junio, 2026 the results, a higher incidence of leukocytosis (p = 0,0127) and neutrophilia (p = 0,0070) was observed, while throm bocytosis was more prevalent in the affected population (n = 37). The most frequent lesions were crusts (n = 52) and ulcers (n = 63), particularly in the nasal plane (p = 0,0185). Ad ditionally, the Feline Grimace Scale was used to measure pain, finding that cats with scores above 4 action units showed a sig nificant correlation with the disease prognosis (p = 0,0024). The findings suggest that the combination of hematological factors, lesion location, and the presence of pain may be de terminants in the prognosis and survival of felines affected by scc","squamous cell carcinoma; leukocytosis; neutro philia; nasal planum; ultraviolet (uv) radiation exposure; pain. Según Thomson [7], la proliferación del carcinoma de células escamosas en la piel de los gatos es del 15 %. Las zonas más afectadas son el plano nasal (80-90 %), el pa bellón auricular (50 %), los párpados (20 %) y la cavidad oral, donde representa entre el 20 y 30 % de los casos [4",marcadores,lente 