2389-9050,(03)-Labor.pdf,(03)-Labor_0,en,amiliar: un estudio,"Introduction Domestic violence is a complex social phenomenon closely linked to labor market instability, structur al inequality, and housing conditions. In Peru, per sistent informality, regional heterogeneity, and the disruptions generated by the COVID-19 pandemic have intensified household stress, making it rele vant to examine how employment dynamics shape gender-differentiated patterns of violence. Objective This study analyzes the relationship between labor market instability and domestic violence across Pe ru’s 25 regions over the period 2009–2023, using a gender-disaggregated approach. The analysis incor porates unemployment, informal labor, overcrowd ing, poverty, education, and access to technology, while capturing persistence, regional disparities, and structural vulnerabilities. Methodology A balanced panel dataset is examined using a two-step System Generalized Method of Moments (GMM) estima tor to address endogeneity and dynamic persistence. The model includes lagged dependent variables and employs internet access and cellphone ownership as instruments for unemployment, strengthening identifi cation under potential reverse causality. Results The results reveal strong temporal persistence in do mestic violence, particularly among men. Unemploy ment shows a negative and significant association with violence against women, but a positive effect for men, suggesting asymmetric responses linked to bargaining power, social roles, and reporting behavior. Informal labor and poverty display patterns consistent with insti tutional constraints, segmentation, and underreporting in more vulnerable regions. Overcrowding operates as a structural stressor amplifying intra-household ten sions, while education and technological access reduce violence by enhancing autonomy, information flows, and access to formal support mechanisms. Conclusions Overall, the findings highlight the need for integrated, gender-sensitive, and regionally targeted policies ad dressing labor market instability and housing condi tions in post-pandemic Peru",gender-based violence; violence; unemployment; in formal sector; labor market; overcrowding; poverty; in formation technology; digital divide; education policy; social protection policy; regional statistics. JEL Classification: J12; J21; J64; C23; O15,marcadores,amiliar un estudio,introduction domestic violence complex social phenomenon closely link labor market instability structur al inequality hous condition peru per sistent informality regional heterogeneity disruption generat covid-19 pandemic have intensifi household stres making rele vant examine how employment dynamic shape gender-differentiat pattern violence objective study analyz relationship between labor market instability domestic violence acros pe ru 25 region over period 2009-2023 using gender-disaggregat approach analysi incor porat unemployment informal labor overcrowd ing poverty education acces technology while captur persistence regional dispariti structural vulnerabiliti methodology balanc panel dataset examin using two-step system generaliz method moment gmm estima tor addres endogeneity dynamic persistence model includ lagg dependent variabl employ internet acces cellphone ownership instrument unemployment strengthen identifi cation under potential reverse causality result result reveal strong temporal persistence do mestic violence particularly among men unemploy ment show negative significant association violence against women but positive effect men suggest asymmetric respons link bargain power social role report behavior informal labor poverty display pattern consistent insti tutional constraint segmentation underreport more vulnerable region overcrowd operat structural stressor amplify intra-household ten sion while education technological acces reduce violence enhanc autonomy information flow acces formal support mechanism conclusion overall finding highlight need integrat gender-sensitive regionally target polici ad dress labor market instability hous condi tion post-pandemic peru,gender-bas violence violence unemployment formal sector labor market overcrowd poverty formation technology digital divide education policy social protection policy regional statistic jel classification j12 j21 j64 c23 o15,amiliar un estudio introduction domestic violence complex social phenomenon closely link labor market instability structur al inequality hous condition peru per sistent informality regional heterogeneity disruption generat covid-19 pandemic have intensifi household stres making rele vant examine how employment dynamic shape gender-differentiat pattern violence objective study analyz relationship between labor market instability domestic violence acros pe ru 25 region over period 2009-2023 using gender-disaggregat approach analysi incor porat unemployment informal labor overcrowd ing poverty education acces technology while captur persistence regional dispariti structural vulnerabiliti methodology balanc panel dataset examin using two-step system generaliz method moment gmm estima tor addres endogeneity dynamic persistence model includ lagg dependent variabl employ internet acces cellphone ownership instrument unemployment strengthen identifi cation under potential reverse causality result result reveal strong temporal persistence do mestic violence particularly among men unemploy ment show negative significant association violence against women but positive effect men suggest asymmetric respons link bargain power social role report behavior informal labor poverty display pattern consistent insti tutional constraint segmentation underreport more vulnerable region overcrowd operat structural stressor amplify intra-household ten sion while education technological acces reduce violence enhanc autonomy information flow acces formal support mechanism conclusion overall finding highlight need integrat gender-sensitive regionally target