household increas inequality gap unpaid work mean higher care-relat expens sumption care responsibiliti reflect high la bour force participation among mother single-par ent household but under less favorable employment condition lower incom which link higher poverty rate conclusion complexity balanc caregiv paid work mother who head single-parent household result increase structural inequality gaps social vulnerability these household household labour economic work mother work time women employment family part time employment informal sector income distribution economic policy economic statistic jel classification b54 j01 j13 j22 d63 sociedad economia ndeg 57 2026 e-issn 2389-9050 e10214770 http doi org 10 25100 sye v0i57 14770 cuidado empleo ingreso en los hogar monomarental,1.0.0,e02369b6c0cbd1cf
2389-9050,(06)-Remote.pdf,(06)-Remote_0,en,zilian Labour Market,"Introduction The COVID-19 pandemic significantly increased the adoption of remote work, creating new dynamics in the labor market. This shift provides a unique op portunity to analyze its impact on long-standing la bor issues, such as gender inequality in the Brazilian context. Objective To examine the effects of the expansion of remote work during the pandemic on gender inequalities in the Brazilian labor market. Methodology Recent data were used to estimate the gender gap in hourly earnings and in the total number of hours worked under remote work arrangements. Addition ally, the extent to which traditional human capital variables explain these disparities was evaluated. Results The findings reveal a significant gap favoring men in both hourly earnings and total hours worked in remote arrangements. A substantial portion of this disparity remains unexplained by traditional human capital variables, indicating the presence of other influencing factors. Conclusions The study concludes that the unexplained portion of the gender gap in remote work is strongly associ ated with the sexual division of domestic labor. This traditional structure continues to penalize women, suggesting that the shift to remote work does not, by itself, mitigate deep-seated gender inequalities in the labor market",Brazil; South America; gender equality; gender di vision of labour; labour market; wages; working conditions; working time; income; women workers; technological change; family environment. JEL Classification: J16; J22; J31,marcadores,zilian labour market,introduction covid-19 pandemic significantly increas adoption remote work creat new dynamic labor market shift provid unique op portunity analyze its impact long-stand la bor issu such gender inequality brazilian context objective examine effect expansion remote work during pandemic gender inequaliti brazilian labor market methodology recent data used estimate gender gap hourly earning total number hour work under remote work arrangement addition ally extent which traditional human capital variabl explain these dispariti evaluat result finding reveal significant gap favor men both hourly earning total hour work remote arrangement substantial portion disparity remain unexplain traditional human capital variabl indicat presence other influenc factor conclusion study conclud unexplain portion gender gap remote work strongly associ ated sexual division domestic labor traditional structure continu penalize women suggest shift remote work does not itself mitigate deep-seat gender inequaliti labor market,brazil south america gender equality gender di vision labour labour market wage work condition work time income women worker technological change family environment jel classification j16 j22 j31,zilian labour market introduction covid-19 pandemic significantly increas adoption remote work creat new dynamic labor market shift provid unique op portunity analyze its impact long-stand la bor issu such gender inequality brazilian context objective examine effect expansion remote work during pandemic gender inequaliti brazilian labor market methodology recent data used estimate gender gap hourly earning total number hour work under remote work arrangement addition ally extent which traditional human capital variabl explain these dispariti evaluat result finding reveal significant gap favor men both hourly earning total hour work remote arrangement substantial portion disparity remain unexplain traditional human capital variabl indicat presence other influenc factor conclusion study conclud unexplain portion gender gap remote work strongly associ ated sexual division domestic labor traditional structure continu penalize women suggest shift remote work does not itself mitigate deep-seat gender inequaliti labor market brazil south america gender equality gender di vision labour labour market wage work condition work time income women worker tech