2389-9980,Cuestiones_v53n120_art2-Augustine+-Hippo's-+Metaphysical-John.pdf,Cuestiones_v53n120_art2-Augustine+-Hippo's-+Metaphysical-John_0,en,"de octubre, 2025","This study examines St. Augustine of Hippo's metaphysical interpretation of John 5:26, explores its philosophical foundations, demonstrates how his argument establishes the Son's equality with the Father, and evaluates its novelty and relevance for contemporary theological scholarship. Within the broader patristic corpus devoted to Trinitarian exegesis, this verse occupies a pivotal place, since it raises the decisive question of how the Son possesses divine life without compromising the Trinity's essential unity or lapsing into Arian subordinationism. The research adopts a comparative qualitative approach, combining textual study of Augustine's Tractates on the Gospel of John with conceptual-philosophical reflection, historical-theological contextualization, and comparative engagement with modern scholarship. The findings confirm that Augustine fundamentally distinguishes between Essential Life, possessed by God alone, and Participatory Life, possessed by creatures in dependence on Him. Crucially, he interprets the phrase ""has granted the Son also to have life in himself"" as a description of the act of eternal generation, whereby the Son is Life itself, equal in essence with the Father, precisely by being from the Father. This demonstrates that Augustine provides a sophisticated metaphysical framework for understanding the mode of the Son's possession of divine life, one that effectively counters subordinationism without relying solely on relational categories. This research contributes to the fields of theology and philosophy by highlighting that Augustine's metaphysical analysis offers a novel perspective often underemphasized in modern scholarship. It offers valuable theological and philosophical resources for articulating the Son's essential equality and deepens theological understanding of the divine nature and Trinitarian relations","Augustine, John 5:26, Trinity, eternal generation, divine metaphysics, patristic theology, exegesis, Essential Life, Participatory Life",marcadores,de octubre 2025,study examin st augustine hippo metaphysical interpretation john 26 explor its philosophical found demonstrat how his argument establish son equality father evaluat its novelty relevance contemporary theological scholarship within broader patristic corpu devot trinitarian exegesi verse occupi pivotal place since rais decisive question how son possess divine life without compromis trinity essential unity laps into arian subordinationism research adopt comparative qualitative approach combin textual study augustine tractat gospel john conceptual-philosophical reflection historical-theological contextual comparative engagement modern scholarship finding confirm augustine fundamentally distinguish between essential life possess god alone participatory life possess creatur dependence him crucially he interpret phrase has grant son also have life himself description act eternal generation whereby son life itself equal essence father precisely being father demonstrat augustine provid sophisticat metaphysical framework understand mode son possession divine life one effectively counter subordinationism without rely solely relational categori research contribut field theology philosophy highlight augustine metaphysical analysi offer novel perspective often underemphasiz modern scholarship offer valuable theological philosophical resourc articulat son essential equality deepen theological understand divine nature trinitarian relation,augustine john 26 trinity eternal generation divine metaphysic patristic theology exegesi essential life participatory life,de octubre 2025 study examin st augustine hippo metaphysical interpretation john 26 explor its philosophical found demonstrat how his argument establish son equality father evaluat its novelty relevance contemporary theological scholarship within broader patristic corpu devot trinitarian exegesi verse occupi pivotal place since rais decisive question how son possess divine life without compromis trinity essential unity laps into arian subordinationism research adopt comparative qualitative approach combin textual study augustine tractat gospel john conceptual-philosophical reflection historical-theological contextual comparative engagement modern scholarship finding confirm augustine fundamentally distinguish between essential life possess god alone participatory life possess creatur dependence him crucially he interpret phrase has grant son also have life himself description act eternal generation whereby son life itself equal essence father precisely being father demonstrat augustine provid sophisticat metaphysical framework understand mode son possession divine life one effectively counter subordinationism without rely solely relational categori research contribut field theology philosophy highlight augustine metaphysical analysi offer novel perspective often underemphasiz modern scholarship offer valuable theological philosophical resourc articulat son essential equality deepen theological understand divine nature trinitarian relation augustine john 26 trinity eternal generation divine metaphysic patristic theology exegesi essential life participatory life,1.0.0,853f2aa01ed4015c
2389-9980,Cuestiones_v53n120_art2-Augustine+-Hippo's-+Metaphysical-John.pdf,Cuestiones_v53n120_art2-Augustine+-Hippo's-+Metaphysical-John_0,es,"al generation, whereby the Son is Life itself, equal in essence with the Father,","Este estudio examina la interpretación metafísica de san Agustín de Hipona sobre Juan 5:26, explora sus fundamentos filosóficos, demuestra cómo su argumentación establece la igualdad del Hijo con el Padre y evalúa su novedad y relevancia para la teología contemporánea. Dentro del amplio corpus patrístico dedicado a la exégesis trinitaria, este versículo ocupa un lugar central, pues plantea la pregunta decisiva sobre el modo en que el Hijo posee la vida divina sin comprometer la unidad esencial de la Trinidad ni caer en el subordinacionismo arriano. La investigación adopta un enfoque cualitativo comparativo que combina el estudio textual de los Tratados sobre el Evangelio de Juan de Agustín con la reflexión conceptual y filosófica, la contextualización histórico-teológica y la comparación con la investigación moderna. Los resultados confirman que Agustín distingue de manera fundamental entre la Vida esencial, que solo pertenece a Dios, y la Vida participada, que poseen las criaturas en dependencia de Él. Además, interpreta de forma decisiva la expresión «ha concedido también al Hijo tener vida en sí mismo» como una descripción del acto de generación eterna, mediante el cual el Hijo es la Vida misma, igual en esencia al Padre, precisamente por proceder de Él. Esto demuestra que Agustín ofrece un marco metafísico sofisticado para comprender el modo en que el Hijo posee la vida divina, el cual refuta eficazmente el subordinacionismo, sin depender únicamente de categorías relacionales. Esta investigación contribuye a los campos de la teología y la filosofía al mostrar que el análisis metafísico de Agustín ofrece una Cuestiones Teológicas, Vol. 53 N.º 120 (Julio-diciembre, 2026) / ISSN: 2389-9980 (en línea) | 3 Life in Himself Through Eternal Generation: St. Augustine of Hippo's Metaphysical Analysis of John 5:26 and Its Relevance perspectiva novedosa, a menudo subestimada por la investigación contemporánea. Aporta valiosos recursos teológicos y filosóficos para articular la igualdad esencial del Hijo y profundiza la comprensión teológica de la naturaleza divina y de las relaciones trinitarias","Agustín, Juan 5:26, Trinidad, generación eterna, metafísica divina, teología patrística, exégesis, Vida esencial, Vida participada",marcadores,generation whereby the son is life itself equal in essence with the father,este estudio examina interpretacion metafisica san agustin hipona sobre juan 26 explora sus fundament filosofic demuestra como argumentacion establece igualdad hijo padre evalua novedad relevancia teologia contemporanea dentro amplio corpus patristico dedicado exegesis trinitaria este versiculo ocupa lugar central pues plantea pregunta decisiva sobre modo hijo posee vida divina sin comprometer unidad esencial trinidad ni caer subordinacionismo arriano investigacion adopta enfoque cualitativo comparativo combina estudio textual tratad sobre evangelio juan agustin reflexion conceptual filosofica contextualizacion historico-teologica comparacion investigacion moderna resultad confirman agustin distingue manera fundamental entre vida esencial solo pertenece dios vida participada poseen criatur dependencia adem interpreta forma decisiva expresion ha concedido tambien hijo tener vida si mismo como descripcion acto generacion eterna mediante cual hijo vida misma igual esencia padre precisa proceder esto demuestra agustin ofrece marco metafisico sofisticado comprender modo hijo posee vida divina cual refuta eficaz subordinacionismo sin depender unica categori relacional esta investigacion contribuye camp teologia filosofia mostrar analisis metafisico agustin ofrece cuestion teologic vol 53 120 julio-diciembre 2026 issn 2389-9980 linea life in himself through eternal generation st augustine of hippo metaphysical a