rango diario crecimiento micelial 14 19 mm d-1 concentracion 24 107 74 107 conidi ml 104 105 clamidospor ml 11 aislad mostraron divers grad actividad inhibitoria contra solani cinco aislad antes mencionad mostraron inhibicion crecimiento micelial variaron 41 64 46 comparacion dos cepas comercial exhibieron tasas inhibicion 20 38 ensay realizad escleroci solani cinco asperellum mencionad cepas comercial demostraron inhibicion germinacion vario 85 100 estos cinco aisl fueron seleccionad como candidat prometedor mayor evaluacion contra solani objetivo desarrollar agente control biologico confiable efectivo uso productor papa region narino adicional costa negra papa solanum tuberosum antagonismo patogen suelo cepas nativ tecnic cultivo dual scleroci received 07-05-2025 accepted 30-06-2025 published 28-08-2025,1.0.0,bb04fd240622e4f9
2422-3719,6_Relationship+between+seedling+traits+and+recovery+time+in+shallot.pdf,6_Relationship+between+seedling+traits+and+recovery+time+in+shallot_0,en,Relationship between seedling traits and recovery time,"Shallot (Allium ascalonicum L.) seedlings frequently experience transplant shock, but the role of seedling traits in recovery dynamics remains unclear. This study investigated three seed-propagated varieties (‘Lokananta’, ‘Merdeka F1’, ‘Sanren F1’) under net-house conditions (June 2024, 5°S, 119°E) using ANOVA, regression, and correlation analyses. Results demonstrated that ‘Merdeka F1’ exhibited superior performance, with 28-42% greater biomass (2.36 g), enhanced root development (12.80 tips, 8.90 cm length), and faster recovery (5.77 days vs. 6.33-6.53 days) compared to other varieties. Pseudostem diameter emerged as the strongest predictor of recovery time (quadratic model: R2=0.77; correlation: r=−0.86), with seedlings >4.5 mm diameter recovering ~1 day faster. Significant negative correlations were also observed between recovery day and biomass (r=−0.66) or leaf count (r=−0.27), while root traits showed minimal influence (R2<0.04). These findings establish pseudostem diameter as a key selection criterion for transplant resilience and highlight ‘Merdeka F1’s genetic advantages. Future research should investigate optimal seedling age and biostimulant applications to enhance shallot transplant success. Additional","Allium ascalonicum L.; transplant shock; seedling vigor; pseudostem diameter; leaf emergence; plant morphology; genotypes. Doi: https://doi.org/10.19053/uptc.24223719.19098 REVISTA COLOMBIANA DE CIENCIAS HORTÍCOLAS - Vol. 19 - No. 3, 1-15, September-December, 2025 e-ISSN: 2422-3719 · ISSN-L: 2011-2173 1 Hasanuddin University – , Agrotechnology Undergraduate Program, Makassar (Indonesia) 2 Hasa",marcadores,relationship between seedl trait recovery time,shallot allium ascalonicum seedling frequently experience transplant shock but role seedl trait 