2422-3794,9_AO+6.pdf,9_AO+6_0,en,clavulanato mostró tendencia ascendente durante y postpandemia.,"Introduction: The COVID-19 pandemic brought about an unprecedented shift in healthcare systems worldwide, impacting both inpatient and outpatient care and altering prescription and dispensing patterns, particularly for antibiotics. This study aimed to determine the effect of the COVID-19 pandemic on the outpatient dispensing of certain antibiotics in a Colombian city between 2018 and 2022. Materials and methods: A retrospective ecological interrupted time series study based on monthly outpatient dispensing records for the antibiotics: amoxicillin, amoxicillin/clavulanate, ampicillin, azithromycin, cephalexin, ciprofloxacin, clarithromycin, doxycycline, norfloxacin, sulfadiazine, trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole, and vancomycin. The defined daily doses per 1,000 inhabitants per day (DHD) were calculated according to World Health Organization guidelines, using the monthly insured population as the denominator. Segmented regression analysis was performed across the pre-pandemic, pandemic, and post-pandemic periods. Results: During the pandemic, the median dispensing rate decreased by 42%, from 4.547 to 2.632 DHD. In the post-pandemic period, it increased to 2.759 DHD but did not reach pre-pandemic levels. A declining trend in dispensing was observed in the pre-pandemic period (−0.122; 95% CI −0.153 to −0.091), with an immediate level change of −1.268 (95% CI −1.824 to −0.711) at the onset of the pandemic, followed by an upward trend during the pandemic (0.149; 95% CI 0.107 to 0.190). Doxycycline (1.114 DHD) and amoxicillin (0.952 DHD) were the most frequently dispensed antibiotics. Amoxicillin/clavulanate showed an increasing trend in dispensing during and after the pandemic. Discussion: Reinforcing surveillance of antimicrobial resistance at the community level is required to understand the effect of differences in dispensing by active ingredient during and after the pandemic","COVID-19; anti-Infective agents; behind-the-counter drugs; drug resistance; microbial; interrupted time series analysis; pharmacoepidemiology L.C. Del Castillo, et al 158 ASOCIACIÓN COLOMBIANA DE INFECTOLOGÍA REVISTA INFECTIO",marcadores,clavulanato mostro tendencia ascendente durante postpandemia,introduction covid-19 pandemic brought about unprecedent shift healthcare system worldwide impact both inpatient outpatient care alter prescription dispens pattern particularly antibiotic study aimed determine effect covid-19 pandemic outpatient dispens certain antibiotic colombian city between 2018 2022 material method retrospective ecological interrupt time seri study based monthly outpatient dispens record antibiotic amoxicillin amoxicillin clavulanate ampicillin azithromycin cephalexin ciprofloxacin clarithromycin doxycycline norfloxacin sulfadiazine trimethoprim sulfamethoxazole vancomycin defin daily dose per 000 inhabitant per day dhd calculat accord world health organ guidelin using monthly insur population denominator segment regression analysi perform acros pre-pandemic pandemic post-pandemic period result during pandemic median dispens rate decreas 42 547 632 dhd post-pandemic period increas 759 dhd but did not reach pre-pandemic level declin trend dispens observ pre-pandemic period -0 122 95 ci -0 153 -0 091 immediate level change -1 268 95 ci -1 824 -0 711 onset pandemic follow upward trend during pandemic 149 95 ci 107 190 doxycycline 114 dhd amoxicillin 952 dhd most frequently dispens antibiotic amoxicillin clavulanate show increas trend dispens during after pandemic discussion reinforc surveillance antimicrobial resistance community level requir understand effect differenc dispens active ingredient during after pandemic,covid-19 anti-infective agent behind-the-counter drug drug resistance microbial interrupt time seri analysi pharmacoepidemiology del castillo et al 158 asociacion colombiana de infectologia revista infectio,clavulanato mostro tendencia 