2422-4200,Logos+18-2_book_v2+(1)-15-35.pdf,Logos+18-2_book_v2+(1)-15-35_0,en,inversa. De estos hallazgos se derivan tres,"Homicidal violence in Colombia exhibits marked territorial heterogeneity that cannot be explained by short-term factors. This article estimates the socioeconomic, demographic, and institutional determinants of the departmental homicide rate using a panel data model for the 2010–2024 period. The dependent variable is the homicide rate per 100,000 inhabitants, selected for its relatively low under-reporting and international comparability. Three dimensions of explanatory variables are incorporated: economic conditions (Gini coefficient, unemployment rate, and monetary poverty), demographic structure (share of young population and population density), and institutional capacity (police force per capita and per capita security expenditure). Pooled, fixed-effects, and random-effects specifications are estimated; the Hausman test (χ² = 18.43; p = 0.010) supports the fixed-effects estimator. Panel diagnostics detect heteroskedasticity, first-order autocorrelation, and cross-sectional dependence, so inference relies on robust standard errors clustered by department. Results show that inequality (β = 48.23; p = 0.004) and unemployment (β = 1.94; p = 0.007) are positively associated with the homicide rate, whereas police presence exerts a significant deterrent effect (β = −0.347; p = 0.007). The effect of monetary poverty is marginal and that of security spending is not robust, consistent with a reverse-causality problem. Three technical elements for policy design follow from these findings: territorial targeting should be guided by each department’s internal inequality trajectory rather than by its comparative level; the police force is an instrument with a measurable effect whereas aggregate spending is not, which locates the problem in the conversion of resources into an effective presence; and security interventions must be articulated with labour-market policy to sustain their effects. Finally, a research agenda focused on property crime is outlined","homicide, social inequality, panel data, institutional capacity, public policy",marcadores,inversa de esto hallazgo se derivan tres,homicidal violence colombia exhibit mark territorial heterogeneity cannot explain short-term factor article estimat socioeconomic demographic institutional determinant departmental homicide rate using panel data model 2010-2024 period dependent variable homicide rate per 100 000 inhabitant select its relatively low under-report international comparability three dimension explanatory variabl incorporat economic condition gini coefficient unemployment rate monetary poverty demographic structure share young population population density institutional capacity police force per capita per capita security expenditure pool fixed-effect random-effect specific estimat hausman test kh2 18 43 010 support fixed-effect estimator panel diagnostic detect heteroskedasticity first-order autocorrelation cross-sectional dependence so inference reli robust standard error cluster department result show inequality 48 23 004 unemployment 94 007 positively associat homicide rate wherea police presence exert significant deterrent effect -0 347 007 effect monetary poverty marginal security spend not robust consistent reverse-causality problem three technical element policy design follow these finding territorial target should guid each department internal inequality trajectory rather than its comparative level police force instrument measurable effect wherea aggregate spend not which locat problem conversion resourc into effective presence security intervention must articulat labour-market policy sustain their effect finally research agenda focus property crime outlin,homicide social inequality panel data institutional capacity public policy,inversa de esto hallazgo se derivan tres homicidal violence colombia exhibit mark territorial heterogeneity cannot explain short-term factor article estimat socioeconomic demographic institutional determinant departmental homicide rate using panel data model 2010-2024 period dependent variable homicide rate per 100 000 inhabitant select its relatively low under-report international comparability three dimension explanatory variabl incorporat economic condition gini coefficient unemployment rate monetary poverty demographic structure share young population population density institutional capacity police force per capita per capita security expenditure pool fixed-effect random-effect specific estimat hausman test kh2 18 43 010 support fixed-effect estimator panel diagnostic detect heteroskedasticity first-order autocorrelation cross-sectional dependence so inference reli robust standard error cluster department result show inequality 48 23 004 unemployment 94 007 positively associat homicide rate wherea police presence exert significant deterrent effect -0 347 007 effect monetary poverty marginal security spend not robust consistent reverse-causality problem three technical element policy design follow these finding territorial target should guid each department internal inequality trajectory rather than its comparative level police force instrument measurable effect wherea aggregate spend not which locat problem conversion resourc into effective presence security intervention must articulat labour-market policy sustain their effect finally research agenda focus property crime outlin homicide social inequality panel data institutional capacity public policy,1.0.0,c488409145c1d897
2422-4200,Logos+18-2_book_v2+(1)-173-189.pdf,Logos+18-2_book_v2+(1)-173-189_0,es,Expansion and Aretaic Deficit in Latin American Police,"La crisis de legitimidad policial en América Latina constituye un fenómeno persistente, marcado por la coexistencia entre expansión normativa y corrupción estructural. Pese al fortalecimiento de los marcos disciplinarios y los dispositivos de control, subsisten dinámicas de captura intrainstitucional que ponen de manifiesto la insuficiencia del paradigma deontológico centrado en el cumplimiento y la sanción. Este enfoque omite una reflexión ontológica sobre el carácter institucional y el juicio prudencial que orienta la discrecionalidad policial. En este contexto, el presente estudio analiza tales limitaciones a partir del caso de la Policía Nacional del Perú, y sostiene que la densificación normativa puede operar como condición facilitadora de la corrupción institucionalizada en las fuerzas de seguridad latinoamericanas. Mediante un estudio de caso instrumental, inscrito en el paradigma interpretativo-hermenéutico y articulado con el análisis crítico del discurso y el análisis temático, el trabajo sugiere que la proliferación normativa genera vacíos de criterio práctico, que la corrupción expresa un déficit aretaico institucional y que la legitimidad del poder coercitivo reclama una ética del carácter fundada en la phronesis. En conclusión, la investigación reconfigura el debate regional al situar la formación moral institucional como condición estructural de legitimidad democrática","policía, ética profesional, corrupción, phronesis, América Latina. 173 Expansión normativa y déficit aretaico en las policías latinoamericanas. La phronesis como condición de legitimidad institucional Revista Logos Ciencia & Tecnología, 18(2), 172-188",marcadores,expansion and aretaic deficit in latin american police,crisis legitimidad policial america latina constituye fenomeno persistente marcado coexistencia entre expansion normativa corrupcion es