2422-4200,Logos+18-2_book_v2+(1)-36-59.pdf,Logos+18-2_book_v2+(1)-36-59_0,en,"os, se observó un","December exhibits distinct patterns of lethal violence; however, detailed empirical evidence from Latin American cities remains limited. This study characterises the temporal, demographic, geographic, and weapon-related patterns of homicides occurring during December in Cali, Colombia, compared with other months (2020–2024) and other major Colombian cities (2016– 2024). An ecological time-series analysis was conducted using data from the Interinstitutional Committee on External Cause Deaths and National Police records. December homicides were compared with those occurring in other months using Z-tests, chi-square tests, Fisher's exact test, and Mann-Kendall trend analyses, with correction for multiple comparisons (Holm and Benjamini-Hochberg), and comparative homicide rates were estimated for Cali, Bogotá, Medellín, Barranquilla, and Colombia. In December 2024, Cali recorded a December rate expressed on an annual basis of 49.4 per 100,000 inhabitants (not equivalent to the observed annual rate), exceeding national and major city averages. Under the Holm procedure, only the concentration of homicides on Fridays (18% vs. 12%) and during the 03:00–11:59 window (30% vs. 23%) remained significant. Exploratory findings, significant only under false discovery rate control, included the increased use of sharp weapons, an interaction between victim sex and weapon type, a rise among victims aged 60–64 years (14 cases), and differences in two comunas; the proportion of coexistencerelated homicides did not differ significantly (20% vs. 17%; p=0.11). These findings describe a differentiated temporal pattern during the Christmas season and underscore the need for targeted, season-specific prevention strategies focused on high-risk times, locations, and populations. The descriptive-comparative design does not allow for causal inference or the evaluation of intervention effectiveness",