2462-8190,BCCM+29(2)_1.pdf,BCCM+29(2)_1_0,es,CENTRO DE MUSEOS,"El Anacardium excelsum (A. excelsum) es una especie de alto valor comercial presente en el bosque seco tropical (bs-T) de Colombia, un ecosistema altamente vulnerable a la variabilidad climática y, además, afectado por la deforestación, la fragmentación del hábitat y la sobreexplotación maderera. Evaluar el crecimiento diamétrico de A. excelsum permite comprender su respuesta ante estas fluctuaciones. Esta investigación analizó el incremento corriente anual (ICA) diamétrico de 23 individuos, durante 11 años, en dos parcelas permanentes de 40 × 50 m, ubicadas en relictos poco intervenidos de bs-T en el norte del departamento de Tolima, en los municipios de Alvarado y Venadillo. En el desarrollo del estudio se formuló un modelo matemático, presentado como Índice de Anomalías Climáticas (IA), con fundamento en las metodologías del Instituto de Hidrología, Meteorología y Estudios Ambientales (IDEAM), integrando anomalías de temperatura y precipitación. Estos datos climáticos provienen de herramientas como el SISDHIM (IDEAM), los modelos hidrológicos CHAC y HEC-HMS y el visor POWER (NASA). El ICA se calculó a partir de diámetros anuales y su relación con el IA se evaluó mediante el método de agrupamiento jerárquico (método de grupos de pares no ponderados con media aritmética – UPGMA), identificando patrones de similitud interanual y agrupaciones de períodos con respuestas comparables. Los resultados mostraron diámetros iniciales entre 11,33 y 112,3 cm y un crecimiento promedio de 0,84 cm año-1, con incrementos altos en los diámetros medios y superiores. El IA presentó una marcada influencia del ENSO, con anomalías de precipitación de hasta +20 % en La Niña y −30 % en El Niño, mientras que las anomalías de temperatura fueron moderadas (−1 a +0,5 °C). El ICA respondió proporcionalmente a estas anomalías, registrando mayor crecimiento en años húmedos y reducciones en períodos secos, sin evidencia de crecimiento diamétrico nulo. El análisis UPGMA reveló patrones recurrentes de respuesta al régimen climático, confirmando que el crecimiento diamétrico de A. excelsum está condicionado principalmente por las fluctuaciones en la disponibilidad hídrica. La metodología empleada permitió caracterizar esta relación, aportando información clave para estrategias de manejo y conservación bajo escenarios de cambio climático","anomalía climática, bosque seco tropical, cambio climático, crecimiento arbóreo, El Niño-Oscilación del Sur (ENSO). 14 bol.cient.mus.hist.nat. 29 (2) julio - diciembre 2025. 13 - 32 Luis Alfredo Lozano-Botache, Omar Aubrelio Melo-Cruz, y Luisa Fernanda Lozano-Castellanos The diametric growth of Anacardium excelsum is affected by local climatic variation in Tolima, Colombia",marcadores,centro muse,anacardium excelsum excelsum especie alto valor comercial presente bosque seco tropical bs-t colombia ecosistema alta vulnerable variabilidad climatica adem afectado deforestacion fragmentacion habitat sobreexplotacion maderera evaluar crecimiento diametrico excelsum permite comprender respuesta ante estas fluctu esta investigacion analizo incremento corriente anual ica diametrico 23 individu durante 11 anos dos parcel permanent 40 50 ubicad relict poco intervenid bs-t norte departamento tolima municipi alvarado venadillo desarrollo estudio formulo modelo matematico presentado como indice anomali climatic ia fundamento metodologi instituto hidrologia meteorologia estudi ambiental ideam integr anomali temperatura precipitacion estos datos climatic provienen herramient como sisdhim ideam model hidrologic chac hec-hms visor power nasa ica calculo partir diametr anual relacion ia evaluo mediante metodo agrupamiento jerarquico metodo grup pares no ponderad media aritmetica upgma identific patron similitud interanual agrup period respuest comparabl resultad mostraron diametr inicial entre 11 33 112 cm crecimiento promedio 84 cm ano-1 increment altos diametr medi superior ia presento marcada influencia enso anomali precipitacion hasta 20 nina -30 nino mientr anomali temperatura fueron moderad -1 degc ica respondio proporcional estas anomali registr mayor crecimiento anos humed reduccion period secos sin evidencia crecimiento diametrico nulo analisis upgma revelo patron recurrent respuesta regimen climatico confirm crecimiento diametrico excelsum esta condicionado principal fluctu disponibilidad hidrica metodologia empleada permitio caracterizar esta relacion aport informacion clave estrategi manejo conservacion bajo escenari cambio climatico,anomalia climatica bosque seco tropical cambio climatico crecimiento arboreo nino-oscilacion sur enso 14 bol cient mus hist nat 29 julio diciembre 2025 13 32 luis alfredo lozano-botache omar aubrelio melo-cruz luisa fernanda lozano-castellan the diametric growth of anacardium excelsum is affected by local climatic variation in tolima colombia,centro muse anacardium excelsum excelsum especie alto valor comercial presente bosque seco tropical bs-t colombia ecosistema alta vulnerable variabilidad climatica adem afectado deforestacion fragmentacion habitat sobreexplotacion maderera evaluar crecimiento diametrico excelsum permite comprender respuesta ante estas fluctu esta investigacion analizo incremento corriente anual ica diametrico 23 individu durante 11 anos dos parcel permanent 40 50 ubicad relict poco intervenid bs-t norte departamento tolima municipi alvarado venadillo desarrollo estudio formulo modelo matematico presentado como indice anomali climatic ia fundamento metodologi instituto hidrologia meteorologia estudi ambiental ideam integr anomali temperatura precipitacion estos datos climatic provienen herramient como sisdhim ideam model hidrologic chac hec-hms visor power nasa ica calculo partir diametr anual relacion ia evaluo mediante metodo agrupamiento jerarquico metodo grup pares no ponderad media aritmetica upgma identific patron similitud interanual agrup period respuest comparabl resultad mostraron diametr inicial entre 11 33 112 cm crecimiento promedio 84 cm ano-1 increment altos diametr medi superior ia presento marcada influencia enso anomali precipitacion hasta 20 nina -30 nino mientr anomali temperatura fueron moderad -1 degc ica respondio proporcional estas anomali registr mayor crecimiento anos humed reduccion period secos sin evidencia crecimiento diametrico nulo analisis upgma revelo patron recurrent respuesta regimen climatico confirm crecimiento diametrico excelsum esta condicionado principal fluctu disponibilidad hidrica metodologia empleada permitio caracterizar esta relacion aport informacion clave estrategi manejo conservacion bajo escenari cambio climatico anomalia climatica bosque seco tropical cambio climatico crecimiento arboreo nino-oscilacion sur enso 14 bol cient mus hist nat 29 julio diciembre 2025 13 32 luis alfredo lozano-botache omar aubrelio melo-cruz luisa fernanda lozano-castellan the diametric growth of anacardium excelsum is affected by local climatic variation in tolima colombia,1.0.0,aff4a9a2c4b8957a
2462-8190,BCCM+29(2)_1.pdf,BCCM+29(2)_1_0,en,"en años húmedos y reducciones en períodos secos, sin evidencia de crecimiento diamétrico nulo. El","Anacardium excelsum is a commercially valuable species found in Colombia's tropical dry forests (bs-T), which are highly vulnerable to climate variability and further impacted by deforestation, habitat fragmentation and overexploitation of timber resources. Assessing its diameter growth provides insight into how it responds to such fluctuations. This study analysed the annual current increment (ACI) in the diameter of 23 individuals over 11 years in two permanent plots measuring 40 x 50 m. These plots were located in minimally disturbed bs-T remnants in the northern department of Tolima, in the municipalities of Alvarado and Venadillo. A Climate Anomaly Index (CAI) was developed based on a mathematical model grounded in IDEAM methodologies, integrating temperature and precipitation anomalies. Data were obtained from the SISDHIM (IDEAM), hydrological models (CHAC and HECHMS) and the NASA POWER viewer. The ACI was calculated from annual diameters and its relationship with the CAI was assessed using the unweighted pair group method with arithmetic mean (UPGMA) to identify patterns of interannual similarity and clusters of periods with comparable responses. Initial diameters ranged from 11.33 to 112.3 cm, and the species exhibited an average growth rate of 0.84 cm per year, with greater increments observed in the medium and large diameter ranges. The CAI exhibited a significant ENSO influence, with precipitation anomalies reaching +20% during La Niña and −30% during El Niño, while temperature anomalies were more moderate at −1 to +0.5 °C. The CAI responded proportionally to these anomalies, showing greater growth in wet years and reductions in dry periods with no evidence of zero diameter growth. UPGMA analysis revealed recurrent response patterns to the climatic regime, confirming that A. excelsum diameter growth is primarily conditioned by fluctuations in water availability. This methodology effectively characterised this relationship, providing key information for management and conservation strategies in the context of climate change","arboreal growth, climatic anomaly, climate variability, El Niño–Southern Oscillation (ENSO), tropical dry forest",marcadores,en anos humedo reduccion en periodo seco sin evidencia de crecimiento diametrico nulo el,anacardium excelsum commercially valuable speci found colombia tropical dry forest bs-t which highly vulnerable climate variability further impact deforestation habitat fragmentation overexploitation timber resourc assess its diameter growth provid insight into how respond such fluctu study analys annual current increment aci diameter 23 individual over 11 year two permanent plot measur 40 50 these plot locat minimally disturb bs-t remnant northern department tolima municipaliti alvarado venadillo climate anomaly index cai develop based mathematical model ground ideam methodologi integrat temperature precipitation anomali data obtain sisdhim ideam hydrological model chac hechm nasa power viewer aci calculat annual diameter its relationship cai assess using unweight pair group method arithmetic mean upgma identify pattern interannual similarity cluster period comparable respons initial diameter rang 11 33 112 cm speci exhibit average growth rate 84 cm per year greater increment observ medium large diameter rang cai exhibit significant enso influence precipitation anomali reach 20 during la nina -30 during el nino while temperature anomali more moderate -1 degc cai respond proportionally these anomali show greater growth wet year reduction dry period no evidence zero diameter growth upgma analysi reveal recurrent response pattern climatic regime confirm excelsum diameter growth primarily condition fluctu water availability methodology effectively characteris relationship provid key information management conservation strategi context climate change,arboreal growth climatic anomaly climate variability el nino-southern oscillation enso tropical dry forest,en anos humedo reduccion en periodo seco sin evidencia de crecimiento diametrico nulo el anacardium excelsum commercially valuable speci found colombia tropical dry forest bs-t which highly vulnerable climate variability further impact deforestation habitat fragmentation overexploitation timber resourc assess its diameter growth provid insight into how respond such fluctu study analys annual current increment aci diameter 23 individual over 11 year two permanent plot measur 40 50 these plot locat minimally disturb bs-t remnant northern department tolima municipaliti alvarado venadillo climate anomaly index cai develop based mathematical model ground ideam methodologi integrat temperature precipitation anomali data obtain sisdhim ideam hydrological model chac hechm nasa power viewer aci calculat annual diameter its relationship cai assess using unweight pair group method arithmetic mean upgma identify pattern interannual similarity cluster period comparable respons initial diameter rang 11 33 112 cm speci exhibit average growth rate 84 cm per year greater increment observ medium large diameter rang cai exhibit significant enso influence precipitation anomali reach 20 during la nina -30 during el nino while temperature anomali more moderate -1 degc cai respond proportionally these anomali show greater growth wet year reduction dry period no evidence zero diameter growth upgma analysi reveal recurrent response pattern climatic regime confirm excelsum diameter growth primarily condition fluctu water availability methodology effectively characteris relationship provid key information management conservation strategi context climate change arboreal growth climatic anomaly climate variability el nino-southern oscillation enso tropical dry forest,1.0.0,63a2661823d5d020
2462-8190,BCCM+29(2)_10.pdf,BCCM+29(2)_10_0,es,"1 Colección Entomológica Universidad de Cusco (CEUC), Laboratorio de Entomología, Facultad de Ciencias","La familia Rhipiceridae es un grupo de coleópteros poco conocido en la región neotropical, con escasos registros documentados en Sudamérica. Hasta el momento, no existían reportes formales de esta familia en el Perú, lo cual representa un vacío significativo en el conocimiento de la biodiversidad del país. En este estudio se describe una nueva especie, Sandalus cuscoensis Yábar-Landa y Bustamante-Navarrete (Rhipiceridae: Coleoptera), procedente del sureste del Perú, que constituye el primer registro documentado de la familia en el territorio nacional. Asimismo, se incorporan observaciones adicionales de Rhipiceridae provenientes de la plataforma de ciencia ciudadana iNaturalist, cuyos datos verificados se integran en el Global Biodiversity Information Facility (GBIF). La combinación de datos taxonómicos y de ciencia ciudadana permite ampliar el conocimiento sobre la distribución y ecología de la familia en la región. Finalmente, se discuten aspectos ecológicos y biogeográficos de los Rhipiceridae en el Perú, destacando la relevancia de este hallazgo para la documentación, investigación y conservación de la biodiversidad entomológica del país","cigarra, Cusco, escarabajos parásitos, iNaturalist, ninfas. COMO CITAR: Yábar-Landa, E., y Bustamante-Navarrete, A.A. (2025). Sandalus cuscoensis Yábar-Landa y BustamanteNavarrete, una nueva especie (Coleoptera: Rhipiceridae) y primer registro documentado de la familia en el Perú. Bol. Cient. Mus. Hist. Nat. Univ. Caldas, 29(2), 203-214. https://doi.org/10.17151/bccm.2025.29.2.10 BOLETÍN CIENTÍF",marcadores,coleccion entomologica universidad cusco ceuc laboratorio entomologia facultad cienci,familia rhipiceridae grupo coleopter poco conocido region neotropical escas registr documentad sudamerica hasta momento no existian report formal esta familia peru cual representa vacio significativo conocimiento biodiversidad pais este estudio describe nueva especie sandalus cuscoensis yabar-landa bustamante-navarrete rhipiceridae coleoptera procedente sureste peru constituye primer registro documentado familia territorio nacional asimismo incorporan observ adicional rhipiceridae provenient plataforma ciencia ciudadana inaturalist cuyos datos verificad integran global biodiversity information facility gbif combinacion datos taxonomic ciencia ciudadana permite ampliar conocimiento sobre distribucion ecologia familia region final discuten aspect ecologic biogeografic rhipiceridae peru destac relevancia este hallazgo documentacion investigacion conservacion biodiversidad entomologica pais,cigarra cusco escarabaj parasit inaturalist ninf como citar yabar-landa bustamante-navarrete 2025 sandalus cuscoensis yabar-landa bustamantenavarrete nueva especie coleoptera rhipiceridae primer registro documentado familia peru bol cient mus hist nat univ cald 29 203-214 https doi org 10 17151 bccm 2025 29 10 boletin cientif,coleccion entomologica universidad cusco ceuc laboratorio entomologia facultad cienci familia rhipiceridae grupo coleopter poco conocido region neotropical escas registr documentad sudamerica hasta momento no existian report formal esta familia peru cual representa vacio significativo conocimiento biodiversidad pais este estudio describe nueva especie sandalus cuscoensis yabar-landa bustamante-navarrete rhipiceridae coleoptera procedente sureste peru constituye primer registro documentado familia territorio nacional asimismo incorporan observ adicional rhipiceridae provenient plataforma ciencia ciudadana inaturalist cuyos datos verificad integran global biodiversity information facility gbif combinacion datos taxonomic ciencia ciudadana permite ampliar conocimiento sobre distribucion ecologia familia region final discuten aspect ecologic biogeografic rhipiceridae peru destac relevancia este hallazgo documentacion investigacion conservacion biodiversidad entomologica pais cigarra cusco escarabaj parasit inaturalist ninf como citar yabar-landa bustamante-navarrete 2025 sandalus cuscoensis yabar-landa bustamantenavarrete nueva especie coleoptera rhipiceridae primer registro documentado familia peru bol cient mus hist nat univ cald 29 203-214 https doi org 10 17151 bccm 2025 29 10 boletin cientif,1.0.0,7d7e1bfcda32b486
2462-8190,BCCM+29(2)_10.pdf,BCCM+29(2)_10_0,en,"e este hallazgo para la documentación, investigación y","The Rhipiceridae family is a little-known group of beetles in the Neotropical region, with few documented records in South America. Until now, there had been no formal reports of this family in Peru, representing a significant gap in knowledge regarding the country's biodiversity. This study describes a new species, Sandalus cuscoensis Yábar-Landa y Bustamante-Navarrete (Rhipiceridae: Coleoptera), from south-eastern Peru. This constitutes the first documented record of the family in the country. Additional observations of Rhipiceridae from the citizen science platform iNaturalist are also included, and their verified data have been integrated into the Global Biodiversity Information Facility (GBIF). Combining taxonomic and citizen science data improves our understanding of the family's distribution and ecology in the region. Finally, the ecological and biogeographical aspects of the Rhipiceridae family in Peru are discussed, emphasising the importance of this discovery for documenting, researching and conserving the country's entomological biodiversity","cicada, Cusco, iNaturalist, nymphs, parasitic beetles",marcadores,este hallazgo para la documentacion investigacion,rhipiceridae family little-known group beetl neotropical region few document record south america until now there had been no formal report family peru represent significant gap knowledge regard country biodiversity study describ new speci sandalu cuscoensi yabar-landa bustamante-navarrete rhipiceridae coleoptera south-eastern peru constitut first document record family country additional observ rhipiceridae citizen science platform inaturalist also includ their verifi data have been integrat into global biodiversity information facility gbif combin taxonomic citizen science data improv our understand family distribution ecology region finally ecological biogeographical aspect rhipiceridae family peru discuss emphasis importance discovery document research conserv country entomological biodiversity,cicada cusco inaturalist nymph parasitic beetl,este hallazgo para la documentacion investigacion rhipiceridae family little-known group beetl neotropical region few document record south america until now there had been no formal report family peru represent significant gap knowledge regard country biodiversity study describ new speci sandalu cuscoensi yabar-landa bustamante-navarrete rhipiceridae coleoptera south-eastern peru constitut first document record family country additional observ rhipiceridae citizen science platform inaturalist also includ their verifi data have been integrat into global biodiversity information facility gbif combin taxonomic citizen science data improv our understand family distribution ecology region finally ecological biogeographical aspect rhipiceridae family peru discuss emphasis importance discovery document research conserv country entomological biodiversity cicada cusco inaturalist nymph parasitic beetl,1.0.0,b1a04b018242f03e
2462-8190,BCCM+29(2)_11.pdf,BCCM+29(2)_11_0,en,Coffee processing affect the strcucture of mayflies assemblages in,"The order Ephemeroptera is widely used as a bioindicator of water quality due to its ecological characteristics, due to this group is ideal for studying the impact of activities like as coffeee hulling on stream ecosystems. Objective: To evaluate the effect of coffee crop on the abundance and diversity of nymphs of Ephemeroptera. Methodology: 2 sites affected by coffee hulling (Palmichal and Guáimaro) and two localities without stressors (reference sites: Libertad and Nuevo mundo) were studied during the months of November and December of 2021. In each site, Ephemeroptera were collected with surber and type D nets in the microhabitats present. Additionally, physicochemical data were taken. To establish the effect of coffee hulling on the order Ephemeroptera, the abundance and richness of genera in disturbed and conserved places were compared. Results: Cluster analysis based on incidence showed the formation of two groups: one constituted by Libertad, Nuevo mundo, Palmichal sites and another by Guáimaro. Likewise, there were significant differences among the abundance of the study sites (ANOSIM R=0.48 p= 0.0007). In the environmentally affected sites Thraulodes and Camelobaetidius dominated in Guáimaro and Leptohyphes and Baetodes in Palmichal, with respect to the reference sites: Baetodes, Leptohyphes, Mayobaetis and Nanomis dominated. Conclusions: The results of the present study showed that the effect of water produced by coffee hulling affects the structure of mayflies, which can be observed in the decreasing of abundance and richness, in the differentiation of dominant taxa, in comparison with reference sites","mayfly, diversity, microhabitats, coffee hulling, enviromental impact. 1 Bióloga, Universidad del Magdalena, Santa Marta, Colombia, carmenvillaloboseb@unimagdalena.edu.co 2 Bióloga, Universidad del Magdalena, Santa Marta, Colombia, gracenunezms@unimagdalena.edu.co Cesar Enrique Tamaris-Turizo 3 Ph.D. Universidad del Magdalena, Santa Marta, Colombia, orcid.org/0000-0002-9600-4641 Google Scholar",marcadores,coffee process affect strcucture mayfli assemblag,order ephemeroptera widely used bioindicator water quality due its ecological characteristic due group ideal study impact activiti like coffeee hull stream ecosystem objective evaluate effect coffee crop abundance diversity nymph ephemeroptera methodology site affect coffee hull palmichal guaimaro two localiti without stressor reference site libertad nuevo mundo studi during month november december 2021 each site ephemeroptera collect surber type nets microhabitat present additionally physicochemical data taken establish effect coffee hull order ephemeroptera abundance richnes genera disturb conserv plac compar result cluster analysi based incidence show formation two group one constitut libertad nuevo mundo palmichal site another guaimaro likewise there significant differenc among abundance study site anosim 48 0007 environmentally affect site thraulod camelobaetidiu dominat guaimaro leptohyph baetod palmichal respect reference site baetod leptohyph mayobaeti nanomi dominat conclusion result present study show effect water produc coffee hull affect structure mayfli which can observ decreas abundance richnes differentiation dominant taxa comparison reference site,mayfly diversity microhabitat coffee hull enviromental impact biologa universidad del magdalena santa marta colombia carmenvillaloboseb unimagdalena edu co biologa universidad del magdalena santa marta colombia gracenunezm unimagdalena edu co cesar enrique tamaris-turizo ph universidad del magdalena santa marta colombia orcid org 0000-0002-9600-4641 google scholar,coffee process affect strcucture mayfli assemblag order ephemeroptera widely used bioindicator water quality due its ecological characteristic due group ideal study impact activiti like coffeee hull stream ecosystem objective evaluate