2462-9448,07+dislipidemia+(2).pdf,07+dislipidemia+(2)_0,en,LDL y Triglicéridos. Conclusiones. Los,"Objective. To compare the clinical outcomes associated with dyslipidemia, both pre- and postoperative, of two bariatric surgery procedures as reported in the scientific literature. Methodology. A meta-analysis of observational studies was conducted following the re commendations of the PRISMA statement and the Cochrane Handbook for Systematic Reviews of Interventions. Digital search strategies were applied to the Embase, PubMed, and Bireme databases. Maximum likelihood restriction models using Hegger’s sets and sub group meta-analyses by follow-up time were employed to synthesize levels of Cholesterol, HDL, LDL, and Triglycerides reported in studies for patients diagnosed with overweight and obesity. Results. Significant findings were identified regarding the variability in lipid profile levels, particularly in interventions such as sleeve gastrectomy. Notable decreases were observed in cholesterol, LDL, and triglycerides, while HDL levels increased. Varia tions were found according to follow-up time, especially at 12 months, in outcomes such as LDL and triglycerides. Conclusions. Bariatric surgery procedures, specifically Rouxen-Y gastric bypass (RYGB) and sleeve gastrectomy (SG), show significant improvements in the lipid profile of patients with obesity and associated comorbidities. Sleeve gastrec tomy appears to be more effective in the long term in increasing HDL levels and reducing LDL levels. The choice of procedure should be based on the specific clinical conditions of each patient. It is crucial to standardize units of measurement and consider postoperative dietary habits to evaluate long-term effectiveness. A multidisciplinary approach and per sonalized educational programs are recommended to promote sustainable healthy changes","Bariatric surgery, Dyslipidemia, Meta-analysis, Lipid profile. Ramírez-Aristizábal, et al. Dislipidemia en pacientes intervenidos con bypass y manga gástrica: Meta-análisis de estudios observacionales 139",marcadores,ldl triglicerido conclusion los,objective compare clinical outcom associat dyslipidemia both pre- postoperative two bariatric surgery procedur report scientific literature methodology meta-analysi observational studi conduct follow re commend prisma statement cochrane handbook systematic review intervention digital search strategi appli embase pubm bireme databas maximum likelihood restriction model using hegger sets sub group meta-analys follow-up time employ synthesize level cholesterol hdl ldl triglycerid report studi patient diagnos overweight obesity result significant finding identifi regard variability lipid profile level particularly intervention such sleeve gastrectomy notable decreas observ cholesterol ldl triglycerid while hdl level increas varia tion found accord follow-up time especially 12 month outcom such ldl triglycerid conclusion bariatric surgery procedur specifically rouxen-y gastric bypas rygb sleeve gastrectomy sg show significant improvement lipid profile patient obesity associat comorbiditi sleeve gastrec tomy appear more effective long term increas hdl level reduc ldl level choice procedure should based specific clinical condition each patient crucial standardize unit measurement consider postoperative dietary habit evaluate long-term effectivenes multidisciplinary approach per sonaliz educational program recommend promote sustainable healthy chang,bariatric surgery dyslipidemia meta-analysi lipid profile ramirez-aristizabal et al dislipidemia en pacient intervenido con bypas manga gastrica meta-analisi de estudio observacional 139,ldl triglicerido conclusion los objective compare clinical outcom associat dyslipidemia both pre- postoperative two bariatric surgery procedur report scientific literature methodology meta-analysi observational studi conduct follow re commend prisma statement cochrane handbook systematic review intervention digital search strateg