2462-991X,10.+Art.+Modificación+de+la+profifilaxis+antibiótica+en+cirugía+impacto+en+la+adherencia+y+las+infecciones+del+sitio+operatorio.pdf,10.+Art.+Modificación+de+la+profifilaxis+antibiótica+en+cirugía+impacto+en+la+adherencia+y+las+infecciones+del+sitio+operatorio_0,en,Modificación de la profilaxis antibiótica,"Introduction: changes to antibiotic prophylaxis protocols directly influence surgical site infection (SSI) rates and overall quality of care. Objective: analyzing the impact of a new antibiotic prophylaxis protocol on healthcare personnel adherence and surgical site infection rates. Materials and Methods: a quasi-experimental (before and after) study, including adult patients who underwent surgical procedures between January 2021 and August 2022 at a tertiary-care hospital in Medellín. Data was collected from the surgery service databases and clinical records. Results: a total of 19.747 surgical interventions were performed (9.102 with the previous protocol and 10.645 with the new protocol), obtaining a 0.7% (n=66) and 0.6% (n=68) SSI rate, respectively (p=0.461). Discussion: organ/space SSIs (56.8%; n=76) were the most common type of SSIs, and most of them occurred in the general surgery service 58.2% (n=78). When comparing the prior protocol with the new protocol, the prior protocol showed greater adherence to antibiotic prophylaxis administration (89.3% vs 88.5%; p=0.881) within the adequate time prior to incision (16.1% vs 5.1%; p=0.05). Rapid surgical procedure initiation stands out (50%) among the causes of non-adherence. Conclusions: findings highlight the importance of ensuring and monitoring adherence to antibiotic prophylaxis in surgical units, while strengthening healthcare personnel. knowledge and feedback","antibiotic prophylaxis, surgical site infection, therapeutic adherence and compliance, antibiotic, surgical wound, treatment protocol, surgery. © 2026 Universidad FUCS. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license (https:/