2463-0470,document (2).pdf,document (2)_0,en,Determinants of labor informality in Antioquia:,"Background and objectives: This study analyzes the determinants of labor informality in the department of Antioquia, considering its economic, social, and territorial implications. It aims to identify the structural and regional factors associated with this phenomenon, as well as the patterns of inequality and vulnerability present across different municipal labor markets. Methods: A descriptive study with a quantitative and spatial analysis approach was conducted. The sample consisted of the municipalities of the department of Antioquia, using secondary data obtained from official sources on employment, productivity, and socioeconomic conditions. The methodological strategy followed a multivariate design based on Multiple Factor Analysis (MFA) and hierarchical clustering techniques to identify municipal typologies, complemented by spatial autocorrelation indicators. Findings: Findings reveal a marked heterogeneity in the configuration of the department’s labor markets. The productive and economic centrality of the Aburrá Valley contrasts with the structural vulnerabilities observed in peripheral regions such as Urabá and Bajo Cauca. Spatial analysis identifies territorial clusters of inequality and limited capacities, showing significant positive autocorrelation among municipalities with similar characteristics. Conclusion: Results suggest that labor informality in Antioquia is driven by interdependent territorial dynamics, where the lack of formal opportunities, low productivity, and insufficient social protection policies interact jointly. Consequently, spatially targeted and territorially differentiated interventions could generate multiplier effects in neighboring areas, contributing to the design of public policies aimed at fostering more equitable and sustainable regional development","Labor informality; Multiple factor analysis; Regional inequality; Spatial analysis; Sustainable development; Territorial clusters. JEL: J46, R23, O18, R12, O15, C38 NUMBER OF REFERENCES 26 NUMBER OF FIGURES 10 NUMBER OF TABLES 6 ORIGINAL RESEARCH PAPER ARTICLE INFO Article History: Received: 15/08/2025 Accepted: 25",marcadores,determinant labor informality antioquia,background objectiv study analyz determinant labor informality department antioquia consider its economic social territorial implic aims identify structural regional factor associat phenomenon well pattern inequality vulnerability present acros different municipal labor market method descriptive study quantitative spatial analysi approach conduct sample consist municipaliti department antioquia using secondary data obtain official sourc employment productivity socioeconomic condition methodological strategy follow multivariate design based multiple factor analysi mfa hierarchical cluster techniqu identify municipal typologi complement spatial autocorrelation indicator finding finding reveal mark heterogeneity configuration department labor market productive economic centrality aburra valley contrast structural vulnerabiliti observ peripheral region such uraba bajo cauca spatial analysi identifi territorial cluster inequality limit capaciti show significant positive autocorrelation among municipaliti similar characteristic conclusion result suggest labor informality antioquia driven interdependent territorial dynamic where lack formal opportuniti low productivity insufficient social protection polici interact jointly consequently spatially target territorially differentiat intervention could generate multiplier effect neighbor area contribut design public polici aimed foster more equitable sustainable regional development,labor informality multiple factor analysi regional inequality spatial analysi sustainable development territorial cluster jel j46 r23 o18 r12 o15 c38 number referenc 26 number figur 10 number tabl original research paper article info article history receiv 15 08 2025 