2619-2551,Incendios+parque+san+cayetano_Met.pdf,Incendios+parque+san+cayetano_Met_0,en,uras superiores a 50 °C y precipitaciones muy,"The analysis of the wildfire that occurred in San Cayetano Provincial Park (PPSC) highlights the significant ecological impact that fire can exert on small-scale protected areas. Within only two days, 79% of the park’s total surface was affected, resulting in substantial loss of grasslands, palm groves, and native forest. Although fire may constitute a natural component of ecological regimes in some regions, in this case, extreme climatic conditions, characterized by prolonged drought and air temperatures exceeding 50 °C, combined with anthropogenic factors to create a critical scenario. These conditions favored the accumulation and desiccation of fine biomass, increasing fuel availability and facilitating rapid-fire spread. Regional studies consistently report an increasing frequency and intensity of such events, particularly under ongoing climate change and intensive land-use pressures. The integration of dronebased aerial surveys and in situ environmental sensors enabled accurate delineation of the burned area and detailed characterization of pre-fire microclimatic conditions, providing essential inputs for restoration and prevention strategies. The results underscore the vulnerability of smallprotected areas to extreme events and highlight the value of integrated approaches based on microclimatic monitoring and remote sensing to support adaptive management planning","Climate change; Microclimate monitoring; Protected areas; Remote sensing; Wildfires. 2 Gilles, D.R.; Contreras, F.I.; Bay-Jouliá, R.; Holzer, C.; Sánchez-Gavier, F.; Kowalewski, M.M.: análisis incendio Parque San Cayetano",marcadores,uras superior 50 degc precipitacion muy,analysi wildfire occurr san cayetano provincial park ppsc highlight significant ecological impact fire can exert small-scale protect area within only two days 79 park total surface affect result substantial loss grassland palm grov native forest although fire may constitute natural component ecological regim some region case extreme climatic condition characteriz prolong drought air temperatur exceed 50 degc combin anthropogenic factor create critical scenario these condition favor accumulation desiccation fine biomas increas fuel availability facilitat rapid-fire spread regional studi consistently report increas frequency intensity such event particularly under ongo climate change intensive land-use pressur integration dronebas aerial survey situ environmental sensor enabl accurate delineation burn area detail character pre-fire microclimatic condition provid essential input restoration prevention strategi result underscore vulnerability smallprotect area extreme event highlight value integrat approach based microclimatic monitor remote sens support adaptive management plann,climate change microclimate monitor protect area remote sens wildfir gill contrera bay-joulia holzer sanchez-gavier kowalewski analisi incendio parque san cayetano,uras superior 50 degc precipitacion muy analysi wildfire occurr san cayetano provincial park ppsc highlight significant ecological impact fire can exert small-scale protect area within only two days 79 park total surface affect result substantial loss grassland palm grov native forest although fire may constitute natural component ecological regim some region case extreme climatic condition characteriz prolong drought air temperatur exceed 50 degc combin anthropogenic factor create critical scenario these condition favor accumulation desiccation fine biomas increas fuel availability facilitat rapid-fire spread regional studi consistently report increas frequency intensity such event particularly under ongo climate change intensive land-use pressur integration dronebas aerial survey situ environmental sensor enabl accurate delineation burn area detail character pre-fire microclimatic condition provid essential input restoration 